window.ETEXTBOOK={"units":[{"id":"u1","title":"古典文学","sections":[{"id":"u1_s1","title":"中国文学的发展","paras":[{"id":"u1_s1_p1","buf":["China has a long and rich literary tradition, but to many Westerners, Chinese literature remains an enigma (谜). This is because much of China’s literary heritage has proved difficult to translate, despite the efforts of scholars."],"text":"China has a long and rich literary tradition, but to many Westerners, Chinese literature remains an enigma (谜). This is because much of China’s literary heritage has proved difficult to translate, despite the efforts of scholars.","chunks":["China has a long and rich literary tradition, but to many Westerners, Chinese literature remains an enigma (谜). This is because much of China’s literary heritage has proved difficult to translate, despite the efforts of scholars."]},{"id":"u1_s1_p2","buf":["For over 3,000 years, Chinese literature has included diverse genres (类型) and forms, such as mythology (神话), poetry, fiction, and drama."],"text":"For over 3,000 years, Chinese literature has included diverse genres (类型) and forms, such as mythology (神话), poetry, fiction, and drama.","chunks":["For over 3,000 years, Chinese literature has included diverse genres (类型) and forms, such as mythology (神话), poetry, fiction, and drama."]},{"id":"u1_s1_p3","buf":["Chinese literature can be divided into three main periods:"],"text":"Chinese literature can be divided into three main periods:","chunks":["Chinese literature can be divided into three main periods:"]},{"id":"u1_s1_p4","buf":["• classical literature: pre-Qin—the May Fourth Movement in 1919"],"text":"• classical literature: pre-Qin—the May Fourth Movement in 1919","chunks":["• classical literature: pre-Qin—the May Fourth Movement in 1919"]},{"id":"u1_s1_p5","buf":["• modern literature: 1919—1949"],"text":"• modern literature: 1919—1949","chunks":["• modern literature: 1919—1949"]},{"id":"u1_s1_p6","buf":["• contemporary literature: 1949—the present"],"text":"• contemporary literature: 1949—the present","chunks":["• contemporary literature: 1949—the present"]},{"id":"u1_s1_p7","buf":["Beginning with primitive mythology, Chinese classical literature developed through works such as The Book of Songs (《诗经》), Odes of Chu (《楚辞》), pre-Qin prose, fu (赋) and yuefu (乐府) poetry of the Han Dynasty, literary criticism in the Wei and Jin dynasties, Tang shi (poetry), Song ci (lyric), Yuan qu (元曲) or Yuan opera, and the novels of the Ming and Qing dynasties."],"text":"Beginning with primitive mythology, Chinese classical literature developed through works such as The Book of Songs (《诗经》), Odes of Chu (《楚辞》), pre-Qin prose, fu (赋) and yuefu (乐府) poetry of the Han Dynasty, literary criticism in the Wei and Jin dynasties, Tang shi (poetry), Song ci (lyric), Yuan qu (元曲) or Yuan opera, and the novels of the Ming and Qing dynasties.","chunks":["Beginning with primitive mythology, Chinese classical literature developed through works such as The Book of Songs (《诗经》), Odes of Chu (《楚辞》), pre-Qin prose, fu (赋) and yuefu (乐府) poetry of the Han Dynasty, literary criticism in the Wei and Jin dynasties, Tang shi (poetry), Song ci (lyric), Yuan qu (元曲) or Yuan opera, and the novels of the Ming and Qing dynasties."]},{"id":"u1_s1_p8","buf":["Modern literature spans the period from 1919 to the founding of the People’s Republic of China in 1949. This period was distinctive as it introduced new literary languages, forms, themes, and techniques. It focused on people’s lives and aspirations, emphasising opposition to imperialism and feudalism, and advocating the building of a new China."],"text":"Modern literature spans the period from 1919 to the founding of the People’s Republic of China in 1949. This period was distinctive as it introduced new literary languages, forms, themes, and techniques. It focused on people’s lives and aspirations, emphasising opposition to imperialism and feudalism, and advocating the building of a new China.","chunks":["Modern literature spans the period from 1919 to the founding of the People’s Republic of China in 1949. This period was distinctive as it introduced new literary languages, forms, themes, and techniques. It focused on people’s lives and aspirations, emphasising opposition to imperialism and feudalism, and advocating the building of a new China."]},{"id":"u1_s1_p9","buf":["Contemporary literature started with the establishment of the PRC in 1949, With rapid economic development and the commitment to reform and opening up, works of various styles and on different subjects appeared. Chinese literature today is flourishing and gaining an important position in world literature."],"text":"Contemporary literature started with the establishment of the PRC in 1949, With rapid economic development and the commitment to reform and opening up, works of various styles and on different subjects appeared. Chinese literature today is flourishing and gaining an important position in world literature.","chunks":["Contemporary literature started with the establishment of the PRC in 1949, With rapid economic development and the commitment to reform and opening up, works of various styles and on different subjects appeared. Chinese literature today is flourishing and gaining an important position in world literature."]}]},{"id":"u1_s2","title":"古典文学","paras":[{"id":"u1_s2_p1","buf":["Chinese classical literature encompasses works produced from the period before the Qin Dynasty up to 1919, representing an unbroken tradition of thought that has endured through dynastic changes."],"text":"Chinese classical literature encompasses works produced from the period before the Qin Dynasty up to 1919, representing an unbroken tradition of thought that has endured through dynastic changes.","chunks":["Chinese classical literature encompasses works produced from the period before the Qin Dynasty up to 1919, representing an unbroken tradition of thought that has endured through dynastic changes."]},{"id":"u1_s2_p2","buf":["Long before written language appeared, there existed an oral tradition of fables and legends. Some of them have been preserved in ancient books as a precious heritage of Chinese literature. Over the course of more than 3,000 years, a great variety of works emerged, including poems, essays, novels, and dramas."],"text":"Long before written language appeared, there existed an oral tradition of fables and legends. Some of them have been preserved in ancient books as a precious heritage of Chinese literature. Over the course of more than 3,000 years, a great variety of works emerged, including poems, essays, novels, and dramas.","chunks":["Long before written language appeared, there existed an oral tradition of fables and legends. Some of them have been preserved in ancient books as a precious heritage of Chinese literature. Over the course of more than 3,000 years, a great variety of works emerged, including poems, essays, novels, and dramas."]}]},{"id":"u1_s3","title":"《诗经》与《楚辞》","paras":[{"id":"u1_s3_p1","buf":["Poetry, one of the earliest art forms in China, originated from folk songs before the advent of written Chinese. Its content was rooted in people’s everyday lives, and reflected their work and entertainment. The Book of Songs and Odes of Chu are regarded as two landmarks in early Chinese literary history."],"text":"Poetry, one of the earliest art forms in China, originated from folk songs before the advent of written Chinese. Its content was rooted in people’s everyday lives, and reflected their work and entertainment. The Book of Songs and Odes of Chu are regarded as two landmarks in early Chinese literary history.","chunks":["Poetry, one of the earliest art forms in China, originated from folk songs before the advent of written Chinese. Its content was rooted in people’s everyday lives, and reflected their work and entertainment. The Book of Songs and Odes of Chu are regarded as two landmarks in early Chinese literary history."]},{"id":"u1_s3_p2","buf":["The Book of Songs is the oldest existing collection of poems or songs, dating from the early Western Zhou Dynasty to the middle of the Spring and Autumn Period. It is regarded as the earliest example of realistic literature in China. As the starting point of Chinese literature, the 305 works in The Book of Songs have provided deep insights into nearly every aspect of social life during the Zhou Dynasty. The volume is revered by scholars and has become an integral part of Chinese cultural heritage. Based on style and tune, the poems can be divided into three sections:"],"text":"The Book of Songs is the oldest existing collection of poems or songs, dating from the early Western Zhou Dynasty to the middle of the Spring and Autumn Period. It is regarded as the earliest example of realistic literature in China. As the starting point of Chinese literature, the 305 works in The Book of Songs have provided deep insights into nearly every aspect of social life during the Zhou Dynasty. The volume is revered by scholars and has become an integral part of Chinese cultural heritage. Based on style and tune, the poems can be divided into three sections:","chunks":["The Book of Songs is the oldest existing collection of poems or songs, dating from the early Western Zhou Dynasty to the middle of the Spring and Autumn Period. It is regarded as the earliest example of realistic literature in China. As the starting point of Chinese literature, the 305 works in The Book of Songs have provided deep insights into nearly every aspect of social life during the Zhou Dynasty. The volume is revered by scholars and has become an integral part of Chinese cultural heritage. Based on style and tune, the poems can be divided into three sections:"]},{"id":"u1_s3_p3","buf":["• feng (ballad): This section contains 160 ballads, which are mostly folk songs representing 15 regions."],"text":"• feng (ballad): This section contains 160 ballads, which are mostly folk songs representing 15 regions.","chunks":["• feng (ballad): This section contains 160 ballads, which are mostly folk songs representing 15 regions."]},{"id":"u1_s3_p4","buf":["• ya (court hymn): This section includes 105 songs performed at court banquets or grand ceremonies, most of which were composed by noblemen."],"text":"• ya (court hymn): This section includes 105 songs performed at court banquets or grand ceremonies, most of which were composed by noblemen.","chunks":["• ya (court hymn): This section includes 105 songs performed at court banquets or grand ceremonies, most of which were composed by noblemen."]},{"id":"u1_s3_p5","buf":["• song (eulogy): This section comprises 40 songs used by the aristocrats for sacrificial ceremonies and to praise emperors."],"text":"• song (eulogy): This section comprises 40 songs used by the aristocrats for sacrificial ceremonies and to praise emperors.","chunks":["• song (eulogy): This section comprises 40 songs used by the aristocrats for sacrificial ceremonies and to praise emperors."]},{"id":"u1_s3_p6","buf":["Many poems in The Book of Songs portray love stories, social customs, and aspects of daily life in ancient China."],"text":"Many poems in The Book of Songs portray love stories, social customs, and aspects of daily life in ancient China.","chunks":["Many poems in The Book of Songs portray love stories, social customs, and aspects of daily life in ancient China."]},{"id":"u1_s3_p7","buf":["The poems in The Book of Songs are primarily written in four-character lines. In terms of literary technique, they can be classified into fu (narrative), bi (analogy), and xing (association). A narrative is a direct reference to an object or an event; an analogy metaphorically likens one thing to another; and an association is an evocative expression of a feeling, mood, or thought, often by using an objective thing as a metaphor for emotions or ideas. All these poems were meant to be sung."],"text":"The poems in The Book of Songs are primarily written in four-character lines. In terms of literary technique, they can be classified into fu (narrative), bi (analogy), and xing (association). A narrative is a direct reference to an object or an event; an analogy metaphorically likens one thing to another; and an association is an evocative expression of a feeling, mood, or thought, often by using an objective thing as a metaphor for emotions or ideas. All these poems were meant to be sung.","chunks":["The poems in The Book of Songs are primarily written in four-character lines. In terms of literary technique, they can be classified into fu (narrative), bi (analogy), and xing (association). A narrative is a direct reference to an object or an event; an analogy metaphorically likens one thing to another; and an association is an evocative expression of a feeling, mood, or thought, often by using an objective thing as a metaphor for emotions or ideas. All these poems were meant to be sung."]},{"id":"u1_s3_p8","buf":["Odes of Chu follows The Book of Songs as another important and influential collection of ancient Chinese poetry. Chuci (《楚辞》), as the book’s Chinese name indicates, is derived from the songs of the southern State of Chu during the Warring States Period. It pioneered romanticism in Chinese poetry. Liu Xiang (刘向) collected and compiled the works of the noted poet Qu Yuan and others into Odes of Chu, which was later annotated and expanded by Wang Yi (王逸)."],"text":"Odes of Chu follows The Book of Songs as another important and influential collection of ancient Chinese poetry. Chuci (《楚辞》), as the book’s Chinese name indicates, is derived from the songs of the southern State of Chu during the Warring States Period. It pioneered romanticism in Chinese poetry. Liu Xiang (刘向) collected and compiled the works of the noted poet Qu Yuan and others into Odes of Chu, which was later annotated and expanded by Wang Yi (王逸).","chunks":["Odes of Chu follows The Book of Songs as another important and influential collection of ancient Chinese poetry. Chuci (《楚辞》), as the book’s Chinese name indicates, is derived from the songs of the southern State of Chu during the Warring States Period. It pioneered romanticism in Chinese poetry. Liu Xiang (刘向) collected and compiled the works of the noted poet Qu Yuan and others into Odes of Chu, which was later annotated and expanded by Wang Yi (王逸)."]},{"id":"u1_s3_p9","buf":["The verses of Odes of Chu are freer than those in The Book of Songs. They are not restricted to the four-character line structure, and the character “兮 (xi)” is often put in the middle or at the end of lines, as shown in Qu Yuan’s famous verse “Long, long is my road, and far, far is the journey; high and low, up and down, I'll search with will. (路漫漫其修远兮，吾将上下而求索。)”"],"text":"The verses of Odes of Chu are freer than those in The Book of Songs. They are not restricted to the four-character line structure, and the character “兮 (xi)” is often put in the middle or at the end of lines, as shown in Qu Yuan’s famous verse “Long, long is my road, and far, far is the journey; high and low, up and down, I'll search with will. (路漫漫其修远兮，吾将上下而求索。)”","chunks":["The verses of Odes of Chu are freer than those in The Book of Songs. They are not restricted to the four-character line structure, and the character “兮 (xi)” is often put in the middle or at the end of lines, as shown in Qu Yuan’s famous verse “Long, long is my road, and far, far is the journey; high and low, up and down, I'll search with will. (路漫漫其修远兮，吾将上下而求索。)”"]},{"id":"u1_s3_p10","buf":["Qu Yuan was the pioneer of Chuci poetry and is considered one of the greatest poets of ancient China. Qu initiated a tradition of romanticism in Chinese literature, his famous verses being studied and memorised by many generations of Chinese people. His immortal masterpieces include Encountering Sorrow (《离骚》), Questioning Heaven (《天问》), Nine Pieces (《九章》), and Nine Songs (《九歌》)."],"text":"Qu Yuan was the pioneer of Chuci poetry and is considered one of the greatest poets of ancient China. Qu initiated a tradition of romanticism in Chinese literature, his famous verses being studied and memorised by many generations of Chinese people. His immortal masterpieces include Encountering Sorrow (《离骚》), Questioning Heaven (《天问》), Nine Pieces (《九章》), and Nine Songs (《九歌》).","chunks":["Qu Yuan was the pioneer of Chuci poetry and is considered one of the greatest poets of ancient China. Qu initiated a tradition of romanticism in Chinese literature, his famous verses being studied and memorised by many generations of Chinese people. His immortal masterpieces include Encountering Sorrow (《离骚》), Questioning Heaven (《天问》), Nine Pieces (《九章》), and Nine Songs (《九歌》)."]},{"id":"u1_s3_p11","buf":["Qu Yuan’s story is largely reflected in his most well-known poem, Encountering Sorrow, which is also his representative work and the longest poem of ancient China. It is a romantic masterpiece."],"text":"Qu Yuan’s story is largely reflected in his most well-known poem, Encountering Sorrow, which is also his representative work and the longest poem of ancient China. It is a romantic masterpiece.","chunks":["Qu Yuan’s story is largely reflected in his most well-known poem, Encountering Sorrow, which is also his representative work and the longest poem of ancient China. It is a romantic masterpiece."]},{"id":"u1_s3_p12","buf":["Qu Yuan was not only celebrated for his many immortal poems; he was also a true patriot. His love for his country and its people is expressed naturally in his poems. Qu Yuan became a heroic example for scholars and officials. Even though he was denied the recognition he deserved, he demonstrated how a person of integrity should behave. Qu Yuan’s legacy of patriotism and perseverance has influenced the country and its people for thousands of years."],"text":"Qu Yuan was not only celebrated for his many immortal poems; he was also a true patriot. His love for his country and its people is expressed naturally in his poems. Qu Yuan became a heroic example for scholars and officials. Even though he was denied the recognition he deserved, he demonstrated how a person of integrity should behave. Qu Yuan’s legacy of patriotism and perseverance has influenced the country and its people for thousands of years.","chunks":["Qu Yuan was not only celebrated for his many immortal poems; he was also a true patriot. His love for his country and its people is expressed naturally in his poems. Qu Yuan became a heroic example for scholars and officials. Even though he was denied the recognition he deserved, he demonstrated how a person of integrity should behave. Qu Yuan’s legacy of patriotism and perseverance has influenced the country and its people for thousands of years."]},{"id":"u1_s3_p13","buf":["The Book of Songs is considered the fountainhead of realism in Chinese literature, while Odes of Chu is seen as the origin of romanticism."],"text":"The Book of Songs is considered the fountainhead of realism in Chinese literature, while Odes of Chu is seen as the origin of romanticism.","chunks":["The Book of Songs is considered the fountainhead of realism in Chinese literature, while Odes of Chu is seen as the origin of romanticism."]},{"id":"u1_s3_p14","buf":["Later Chinese literature has expanded upon and developed these two creative traditions."],"text":"Later Chinese literature has expanded upon and developed these two creative traditions.","chunks":["Later Chinese literature has expanded upon and developed these two creative traditions."]}]},{"id":"u1_s4","title":"先秦散文","paras":[{"id":"u1_s4_p1","buf":["The formation of ancient Chinese prose is marked by Classic of History (《尚书》), a collection of documentary records of events in China’s ancient history. After Classic of History, prose literature developed in two directions: historical prose (历史散文), which focused on narration, and philosophers’ prose (诸子散文) which comprised the writings of various scholars."],"text":"The formation of ancient Chinese prose is marked by Classic of History (《尚书》), a collection of documentary records of events in China’s ancient history. After Classic of History, prose literature developed in two directions: historical prose (历史散文), which focused on narration, and philosophers’ prose (诸子散文) which comprised the writings of various scholars.","chunks":["The formation of ancient Chinese prose is marked by Classic of History (《尚书》), a collection of documentary records of events in China’s ancient history. After Classic of History, prose literature developed in two directions: historical prose (历史散文), which focused on narration, and philosophers’ prose (诸子散文) which comprised the writings of various scholars."]}]},{"id":"u1_s5","title":"历史散文","paras":[{"id":"u1_s5_p1","buf":["There are four main representative works of historical prose:"],"text":"There are four main representative works of historical prose:","chunks":["There are four main representative works of historical prose:"]},{"id":"u1_s5_p2","buf":["• The Spring and Autumn Annals (《春秋》): It is the earliest chronicle (编年史) in China with concise and refined language."],"text":"• The Spring and Autumn Annals (《春秋》): It is the earliest chronicle (编年史) in China with concise and refined language.","chunks":["• The Spring and Autumn Annals (《春秋》): It is the earliest chronicle (编年史) in China with concise and refined language."]},{"id":"u1_s5_p3","buf":["• Zuo Qiuming’s Commentary on The Spring and Autumn Annals (《左传》): As The Spring and Autumn Annals was later incorporated into the classics (jing), books that interpreted the classics were called commentaries (zhuan). Among the three famous commentaries on The Spring and Autumn Annals, Zuo Qiuming’s Commentary on The Spring and Autumn Annals is the first comprehensive historical account of the major political, social, and military events of the Spring and Autumn Period."],"text":"• Zuo Qiuming’s Commentary on The Spring and Autumn Annals (《左传》): As The Spring and Autumn Annals was later incorporated into the classics (jing), books that interpreted the classics were called commentaries (zhuan). Among the three famous commentaries on The Spring and Autumn Annals, Zuo Qiuming’s Commentary on The Spring and Autumn Annals is the first comprehensive historical account of the major political, social, and military events of the Spring and Autumn Period.","chunks":["• Zuo Qiuming’s Commentary on The Spring and Autumn Annals (《左传》): As The Spring and Autumn Annals was later incorporated into the classics (jing), books that interpreted the classics were called commentaries (zhuan). Among the three famous commentaries on The Spring and Autumn Annals, Zuo Qiuming’s Commentary on The Spring and Autumn Annals is the first comprehensive historical account of the major political, social, and military events of the Spring and Autumn Period."]},{"id":"u1_s5_p4","buf":["• Discourses on Governance of the States (《国语》): It is a collection of historical articles about figures, events, and sayings from eight states."],"text":"• Discourses on Governance of the States (《国语》): It is a collection of historical articles about figures, events, and sayings from eight states.","chunks":["• Discourses on Governance of the States (《国语》): It is a collection of historical articles about figures, events, and sayings from eight states."]},{"id":"u1_s5_p5","buf":["• Strategies of the Warring States (《战国策》): It is a historical work recording the history of the various states during the pre-Qin period, focusing on political strategies and warfare."],"text":"• Strategies of the Warring States (《战国策》): It is a historical work recording the history of the various states during the pre-Qin period, focusing on political strategies and warfare.","chunks":["• Strategies of the Warring States (《战国策》): It is a historical work recording the history of the various states during the pre-Qin period, focusing on political strategies and warfare."]}]},{"id":"u1_s6","title":"诸子散文","paras":[{"id":"u1_s6_p1","buf":["During the Spring and Autumn Period and the Warring States Period, Chinese society experienced radical changes. Various schools of thought, such as Confucianism, Mohism, Daoism, and Legalism, all strove to promote their ideas by writing books and expounding (阐述) their theories. Some famous pieces of philosophers’ prose include:"],"text":"During the Spring and Autumn Period and the Warring States Period, Chinese society experienced radical changes. Various schools of thought, such as Confucianism, Mohism, Daoism, and Legalism, all strove to promote their ideas by writing books and expounding (阐述) their theories. Some famous pieces of philosophers’ prose include:","chunks":["During the Spring and Autumn Period and the Warring States Period, Chinese society experienced radical changes. Various schools of thought, such as Confucianism, Mohism, Daoism, and Legalism, all strove to promote their ideas by writing books and expounding (阐述) their theories. Some famous pieces of philosophers’ prose include:"]},{"id":"u1_s6_p2","buf":["• The Analects: Compiled by the disciples of Confucius, this work records his sayings and behaviour in a question-and-answer format. It made terseness (简洁) and profundity (深奥) the foremost criteria for prose style."],"text":"• The Analects: Compiled by the disciples of Confucius, this work records his sayings and behaviour in a question-and-answer format. It made terseness (简洁) and profundity (深奥) the foremost criteria for prose style.","chunks":["• The Analects: Compiled by the disciples of Confucius, this work records his sayings and behaviour in a question-and-answer format. It made terseness (简洁) and profundity (深奥) the foremost criteria for prose style."]},{"id":"u1_s6_p3","buf":["• Daodejing: Written by Laozi, it is regarded as the scripture of Daoism. It uses rhyme (韵) similar to prose poems and is imbued with philosophical ideas. It centres on dao, the principle underlying and governing all things in existence."],"text":"• Daodejing: Written by Laozi, it is regarded as the scripture of Daoism. It uses rhyme (韵) similar to prose poems and is imbued with philosophical ideas. It centres on dao, the principle underlying and governing all things in existence.","chunks":["• Daodejing: Written by Laozi, it is regarded as the scripture of Daoism. It uses rhyme (韵) similar to prose poems and is imbued with philosophical ideas. It centres on dao, the principle underlying and governing all things in existence."]},{"id":"u1_s6_p4","buf":["• Mozi: This work is known for its extensive and effective use of logical reasoning and argumentation."],"text":"• Mozi: This work is known for its extensive and effective use of logical reasoning and argumentation.","chunks":["• Mozi: This work is known for its extensive and effective use of logical reasoning and argumentation."]},{"id":"u1_s6_p5","buf":["• Mencius: It makes full use of clear and fluent language to create an eloquent (雄辩的) and persuasive style."],"text":"• Mencius: It makes full use of clear and fluent language to create an eloquent (雄辩的) and persuasive style.","chunks":["• Mencius: It makes full use of clear and fluent language to create an eloquent (雄辩的) and persuasive style."]},{"id":"u1_s6_p6","buf":["• Zhuangzi: This work has established a model of highly imaginative prose that defies classification."],"text":"• Zhuangzi: This work has established a model of highly imaginative prose that defies classification.","chunks":["• Zhuangzi: This work has established a model of highly imaginative prose that defies classification."]},{"id":"u1_s6_p7","buf":["These works became sources and models for later literature, both spoken and written."],"text":"These works became sources and models for later literature, both spoken and written.","chunks":["These works became sources and models for later literature, both spoken and written."]}]},{"id":"u1_s7","title":"汉代文学","paras":[{"id":"u1_s7_p1","buf":["The most notable achievements of the literature in the Han Dynasty are its fu and yuefu poetry."],"text":"The most notable achievements of the literature in the Han Dynasty are its fu and yuefu poetry.","chunks":["The most notable achievements of the literature in the Han Dynasty are its fu and yuefu poetry."]},{"id":"u1_s7_p2","buf":["Han fu was a popular literary genre in the Han Dynasty. It emphasises elaboration and description, combining elements of poetry and prose, and is also known as rhymed prose. Han fu can be divided into three main types: saoti fu (骚体赋), da fu (大赋), and lyrical xiao fu (抒情小赋), Saoti fu inherits the form and style of the poem Encountering Sorrow by Qu Yuan. Da fu focuses on the detailed description of objects, aims to express the author’s aspirations, and often serves as an allegory or counsel. Its most prominent feature is its extravagant depiction with abundant use of ornate rhetoric. Lyrical xiao fu became popular in the middle of the Eastern Han Dynasty and focused on expressing personal feelings and aspirations."],"text":"Han fu was a popular literary genre in the Han Dynasty. It emphasises elaboration and description, combining elements of poetry and prose, and is also known as rhymed prose. Han fu can be divided into three main types: saoti fu (骚体赋), da fu (大赋), and lyrical xiao fu (抒情小赋), Saoti fu inherits the form and style of the poem Encountering Sorrow by Qu Yuan. Da fu focuses on the detailed description of objects, aims to express the author’s aspirations, and often serves as an allegory or counsel. Its most prominent feature is its extravagant depiction with abundant use of ornate rhetoric. Lyrical xiao fu became popular in the middle of the Eastern Han Dynasty and focused on expressing personal feelings and aspirations.","chunks":["Han fu was a popular literary genre in the Han Dynasty. It emphasises elaboration and description, combining elements of poetry and prose, and is also known as rhymed prose. Han fu can be divided into three main types: saoti fu (骚体赋), da fu (大赋), and lyrical xiao fu (抒情小赋), Saoti fu inherits the form and style of the poem Encountering Sorrow by Qu Yuan. Da fu focuses on the detailed description of objects, aims to express the author’s aspirations, and often serves as an allegory or counsel. Its most prominent feature is its extravagant depiction with abundant use of ornate rhetoric. Lyrical xiao fu became popular in the middle of the Eastern Han Dynasty and focused on expressing personal feelings and aspirations."]},{"id":"u1_s7_p3","buf":["The most notable Han fu writers include Jia Yi (贾谊) and Sima Xiangru (司马相如). Jia’s representative works are “Fu of Eulogy on Qu Yuan”(《吊屈原赋》) and “Funiao Fu” (《鵩鸟赋》). Sima’s major work is “Fu on the Excursion Hunt of the Son of Heaven” (《天子游猎赋》)."],"text":"The most notable Han fu writers include Jia Yi (贾谊) and Sima Xiangru (司马相如). Jia’s representative works are “Fu of Eulogy on Qu Yuan”(《吊屈原赋》) and “Funiao Fu” (《鵩鸟赋》). Sima’s major work is “Fu on the Excursion Hunt of the Son of Heaven” (《天子游猎赋》).","chunks":["The most notable Han fu writers include Jia Yi (贾谊) and Sima Xiangru (司马相如). Jia’s representative works are “Fu of Eulogy on Qu Yuan”(《吊屈原赋》) and “Funiao Fu” (《鵩鸟赋》). Sima’s major work is “Fu on the Excursion Hunt of the Son of Heaven” (《天子游猎赋》)."]},{"id":"u1_s7_p4","buf":["Sima Qian (司马迁) was the first historian in Chinese history to record and narrate historical events in the form of individual biographies. Records of the Grand Historian (《史记》) is a monumental work that records Chinese history from the legendary Huangdi to the Han Dynasty, covering a period of about 3,000 years in 130 chapters. Sima Qian employed five styles in writing the book:"],"text":"Sima Qian (司马迁) was the first historian in Chinese history to record and narrate historical events in the form of individual biographies. Records of the Grand Historian (《史记》) is a monumental work that records Chinese history from the legendary Huangdi to the Han Dynasty, covering a period of about 3,000 years in 130 chapters. Sima Qian employed five styles in writing the book:","chunks":["Sima Qian (司马迁) was the first historian in Chinese history to record and narrate historical events in the form of individual biographies. Records of the Grand Historian (《史记》) is a monumental work that records Chinese history from the legendary Huangdi to the Han Dynasty, covering a period of about 3,000 years in 130 chapters. Sima Qian employed five styles in writing the book:"]},{"id":"u1_s7_p5","buf":["• benji (本纪): biographies of prominent rulers and emperors;"],"text":"• benji (本纪): biographies of prominent rulers and emperors;","chunks":["• benji (本纪): biographies of prominent rulers and emperors;"]},{"id":"u1_s7_p6","buf":["• biao (表): chronological tables;"],"text":"• biao (表): chronological tables;","chunks":["• biao (表): chronological tables;"]},{"id":"u1_s7_p7","buf":["• shu (书): special discussions on important legal, institutional, economic, and cultural topics;"],"text":"• shu (书): special discussions on important legal, institutional, economic, and cultural topics;","chunks":["• shu (书): special discussions on important legal, institutional, economic, and cultural topics;"]},{"id":"u1_s7_p8","buf":["• shijia (世家): biographies of notable rulers, nobles, and bureaucrats;"],"text":"• shijia (世家): biographies of notable rulers, nobles, and bureaucrats;","chunks":["• shijia (世家): biographies of notable rulers, nobles, and bureaucrats;"]},{"id":"u1_s7_p9","buf":["• liezhuan (列传): biographies of important figures from various social classes. Later official historians followed these styles."],"text":"• liezhuan (列传): biographies of important figures from various social classes. Later official historians followed these styles.","chunks":["• liezhuan (列传): biographies of important figures from various social classes. Later official historians followed these styles."]},{"id":"u1_s7_p10","buf":["Yuefu poems are mostly written in five-character lines, although some use seven-character or multi-character lines. They reflect various aspects of society and the lives of ordinary people at the time."],"text":"Yuefu poems are mostly written in five-character lines, although some use seven-character or multi-character lines. They reflect various aspects of society and the lives of ordinary people at the time.","chunks":["Yuefu poems are mostly written in five-character lines, although some use seven-character or multi-character lines. They reflect various aspects of society and the lives of ordinary people at the time."]},{"id":"u1_s7_p11","buf":["These folk songs feature natural language and vivid depictions of the lives of ordinary people, especially women. Through the use of metaphors and personification, the stories in these folk songs come vividly to life. The most famous of these works is “The Peacock Flies Southeast” (《孔雀东南飞》), one of the longest ballads from the end of the Eastern Han Dynasty. It tells the tragic story of a young couple, Jiao Zhongqing (焦仲卿) and Liu Lanzhi (刘兰芝), and is often interpreted as a critique of feudal ethics."],"text":"These folk songs feature natural language and vivid depictions of the lives of ordinary people, especially women. Through the use of metaphors and personification, the stories in these folk songs come vividly to life. The most famous of these works is “The Peacock Flies Southeast” (《孔雀东南飞》), one of the longest ballads from the end of the Eastern Han Dynasty. It tells the tragic story of a young couple, Jiao Zhongqing (焦仲卿) and Liu Lanzhi (刘兰芝), and is often interpreted as a critique of feudal ethics.","chunks":["These folk songs feature natural language and vivid depictions of the lives of ordinary people, especially women. Through the use of metaphors and personification, the stories in these folk songs come vividly to life. The most famous of these works is “The Peacock Flies Southeast” (《孔雀东南飞》), one of the longest ballads from the end of the Eastern Han Dynasty. It tells the tragic story of a young couple, Jiao Zhongqing (焦仲卿) and Liu Lanzhi (刘兰芝), and is often interpreted as a critique of feudal ethics."]}]},{"id":"u1_s8","title":"魏晋南北朝文学","paras":[{"id":"u1_s8_p1","buf":["Chinese literature during these dynasties was notable for its literary criticism. Additionally, anecdotal tales and supernatural tales (志人志怪小说) became a recognised genre."],"text":"Chinese literature during these dynasties was notable for its literary criticism. Additionally, anecdotal tales and supernatural tales (志人志怪小说) became a recognised genre.","chunks":["Chinese literature during these dynasties was notable for its literary criticism. Additionally, anecdotal tales and supernatural tales (志人志怪小说) became a recognised genre."]},{"id":"u1_s8_p2","buf":["The group known as the “Three Caos (三曹)” includes Cao Cao, Cao Pi, and Cao Zhi; the “Seven Masters of the Jian’an Period (建安七子)” include Kong Rong, Chen Lin, Wang Can, Xu Gan, Ruan Yu, Ying Yang, and Liu Zhen. They were outstanding literary figures. Their poems captured the spirit of the time, invoking an ambience (氛围) of heroism and sadness, and created a style later referred to as “Jian’an literary style (建安风骨)” Cao Cao was deeply influenced by The Book of Songs. In “A Short Song” (《短歌行》), he directly references lines from The Book of Songs to express the urgent desire for talents and his ambition. “The Sea\"”(《观沧海》) is another well-known piece of Cao Cao."],"text":"The group known as the “Three Caos (三曹)” includes Cao Cao, Cao Pi, and Cao Zhi; the “Seven Masters of the Jian’an Period (建安七子)” include Kong Rong, Chen Lin, Wang Can, Xu Gan, Ruan Yu, Ying Yang, and Liu Zhen. They were outstanding literary figures. Their poems captured the spirit of the time, invoking an ambience (氛围) of heroism and sadness, and created a style later referred to as “Jian’an literary style (建安风骨)” Cao Cao was deeply influenced by The Book of Songs. In “A Short Song” (《短歌行》), he directly references lines from The Book of Songs to express the urgent desire for talents and his ambition. “The Sea\"”(《观沧海》) is another well-known piece of Cao Cao.","chunks":["The group known as the “Three Caos (三曹)” includes Cao Cao, Cao Pi, and Cao Zhi; the “Seven Masters of the Jian’an Period (建安七子)” include Kong Rong, Chen Lin, Wang Can, Xu Gan, Ruan Yu, Ying Yang, and Liu Zhen. They were outstanding literary figures. Their poems captured the spirit of the time, invoking an ambience (氛围) of heroism and sadness, and created a style later referred to as “Jian’an literary style (建安风骨)” Cao Cao was deeply influenced by The Book of Songs. In “A Short Song” (《短歌行》), he directly references lines from The Book of Songs to express the urgent desire for talents and his ambition. “The Sea\"”(《观沧海》) is another well-known piece of Cao Cao."]},{"id":"u1_s8_p3","buf":["Tao Yuanming was a master of five-character-per-line poetry. His five-character poems can be divided into two categories: yonghuai poems, which express personal feelings and aspirations, and pastoral poems. Tao Yuanming’s poetic achievements are mainly manifested in his pastoral poetry, which is characterised by its simplicity, tranquillity, and understated beauty. Tao’s masterpiece “Peach Blossom Spring”(《桃花源记》) is celebrated for its depiction of the secluded and ideal life people long for."],"text":"Tao Yuanming was a master of five-character-per-line poetry. His five-character poems can be divided into two categories: yonghuai poems, which express personal feelings and aspirations, and pastoral poems. Tao Yuanming’s poetic achievements are mainly manifested in his pastoral poetry, which is characterised by its simplicity, tranquillity, and understated beauty. Tao’s masterpiece “Peach Blossom Spring”(《桃花源记》) is celebrated for its depiction of the secluded and ideal life people long for.","chunks":["Tao Yuanming was a master of five-character-per-line poetry. His five-character poems can be divided into two categories: yonghuai poems, which express personal feelings and aspirations, and pastoral poems. Tao Yuanming’s poetic achievements are mainly manifested in his pastoral poetry, which is characterised by its simplicity, tranquillity, and understated beauty. Tao’s masterpiece “Peach Blossom Spring”(《桃花源记》) is celebrated for its depiction of the secluded and ideal life people long for."]},{"id":"u1_s8_p4","buf":["The Literary Mind and the Carving of Dragons (《文心雕龙》) written by Liu Xie (刘勰) in parallel prose (骈文), is a systematic, well-structured, and detailed treatise on Chinese literature. Liu Xie wrote this book to critique his contemporaries’ excessive focus on stylistic beauty at the cost of meaningful content. He advocated for the inclusion of social and political substance in literature."],"text":"The Literary Mind and the Carving of Dragons (《文心雕龙》) written by Liu Xie (刘勰) in parallel prose (骈文), is a systematic, well-structured, and detailed treatise on Chinese literature. Liu Xie wrote this book to critique his contemporaries’ excessive focus on stylistic beauty at the cost of meaningful content. He advocated for the inclusion of social and political substance in literature.","chunks":["The Literary Mind and the Carving of Dragons (《文心雕龙》) written by Liu Xie (刘勰) in parallel prose (骈文), is a systematic, well-structured, and detailed treatise on Chinese literature. Liu Xie wrote this book to critique his contemporaries’ excessive focus on stylistic beauty at the cost of meaningful content. He advocated for the inclusion of social and political substance in literature."]}]},{"id":"u1_s9","title":"唐诗","paras":[{"id":"u1_s9_p1","buf":["The Tang Dynasty witnessed the continued development of Chinese poetry. The Complete Poetry of the Tang Dynasty (《全唐诗》), compiled in the Qing Dynasty, is a large collection of celebrated Chinese poetry. It contains more than 48,900 poems by over 2,200 poets in various styles."],"text":"The Tang Dynasty witnessed the continued development of Chinese poetry. The Complete Poetry of the Tang Dynasty (《全唐诗》), compiled in the Qing Dynasty, is a large collection of celebrated Chinese poetry. It contains more than 48,900 poems by over 2,200 poets in various styles.","chunks":["The Tang Dynasty witnessed the continued development of Chinese poetry. The Complete Poetry of the Tang Dynasty (《全唐诗》), compiled in the Qing Dynasty, is a large collection of celebrated Chinese poetry. It contains more than 48,900 poems by over 2,200 poets in various styles."]},{"id":"u1_s9_p2","buf":["The development of poetry during the Tang Dynasty can be classified into four stages: early Tang, high Tang, mid-Tang, and late Tang."],"text":"The development of poetry during the Tang Dynasty can be classified into four stages: early Tang, high Tang, mid-Tang, and late Tang.","chunks":["The development of poetry during the Tang Dynasty can be classified into four stages: early Tang, high Tang, mid-Tang, and late Tang."]},{"id":"u1_s9_p3","buf":["The four pre-eminent (初唐四杰) poets of the early Tang period, Wang Bo, Yang Jiong, Lu Zhaolin, and Luo Binwang, are known as the “Four Talents in Early Tang Dynasty (初唐四杰)” and are regarded as important figures in the development of early Tang poetry. Their poems are characterised by refined language, innovative tonal patterns, and elegant rhyming schemes. The lines written by Wang Bo, “A bosom friend afar brings a distant land near (海内存知己，天涯若比邻)”help to relieve the lingering sorrow of departure and have become a famous Saying to express friendship in later years."],"text":"The four pre-eminent (初唐四杰) poets of the early Tang period, Wang Bo, Yang Jiong, Lu Zhaolin, and Luo Binwang, are known as the “Four Talents in Early Tang Dynasty (初唐四杰)” and are regarded as important figures in the development of early Tang poetry. Their poems are characterised by refined language, innovative tonal patterns, and elegant rhyming schemes. The lines written by Wang Bo, “A bosom friend afar brings a distant land near (海内存知己，天涯若比邻)”help to relieve the lingering sorrow of departure and have become a famous Saying to express friendship in later years.","chunks":["The four pre-eminent (初唐四杰) poets of the early Tang period, Wang Bo, Yang Jiong, Lu Zhaolin, and Luo Binwang, are known as the “Four Talents in Early Tang Dynasty (初唐四杰)” and are regarded as important figures in the development of early Tang poetry. Their poems are characterised by refined language, innovative tonal patterns, and elegant rhyming schemes. The lines written by Wang Bo, “A bosom friend afar brings a distant land near (海内存知己，天涯若比邻)”help to relieve the lingering sorrow of departure and have become a famous Saying to express friendship in later years."]},{"id":"u1_s9_p4","buf":["A whole generation of literary giants emerged during the high Tang period, a time marked by prosperity and stability in society. Among them, Li Bai and Du Fu are regarded as the most representative poets of the Tang Dynasty."],"text":"A whole generation of literary giants emerged during the high Tang period, a time marked by prosperity and stability in society. Among them, Li Bai and Du Fu are regarded as the most representative poets of the Tang Dynasty.","chunks":["A whole generation of literary giants emerged during the high Tang period, a time marked by prosperity and stability in society. Among them, Li Bai and Du Fu are regarded as the most representative poets of the Tang Dynasty."]},{"id":"u1_s9_p5","buf":["Li Bai, known as the “Poet Immortal (诗仙)” has long been considered the greatest poet of the romantic tradition in Chinese literature. In his poems, imagination, exaggeration, diction, and melodious (悦耳的) rhythms are blended effortlessly. Some of Li Bai’s most famous poems include “Quiet Night Thoughts” (《静夜思》), “Hard Is the Road to Shu” (《蜀道难》), and “A Visit to Tianmu Mountain ina Dream” (《梦游天姥吟留别》)."],"text":"Li Bai, known as the “Poet Immortal (诗仙)” has long been considered the greatest poet of the romantic tradition in Chinese literature. In his poems, imagination, exaggeration, diction, and melodious (悦耳的) rhythms are blended effortlessly. Some of Li Bai’s most famous poems include “Quiet Night Thoughts” (《静夜思》), “Hard Is the Road to Shu” (《蜀道难》), and “A Visit to Tianmu Mountain ina Dream” (《梦游天姥吟留别》).","chunks":["Li Bai, known as the “Poet Immortal (诗仙)” has long been considered the greatest poet of the romantic tradition in Chinese literature. In his poems, imagination, exaggeration, diction, and melodious (悦耳的) rhythms are blended effortlessly. Some of Li Bai’s most famous poems include “Quiet Night Thoughts” (《静夜思》), “Hard Is the Road to Shu” (《蜀道难》), and “A Visit to Tianmu Mountain ina Dream” (《梦游天姥吟留别》)."]},{"id":"u1_s9_p6","buf":["Du Fu, known as the “Sage of Poetry (诗圣)” is regarded as the greatest cel poet of realism in Chinese literature. Serving as a mirror of his times, his poems faithfully and profoundly reflect the social realities of the declining Tang Dynasty, functioning as poetic records of history."],"text":"Du Fu, known as the “Sage of Poetry (诗圣)” is regarded as the greatest cel poet of realism in Chinese literature. Serving as a mirror of his times, his poems faithfully and profoundly reflect the social realities of the declining Tang Dynasty, functioning as poetic records of history.","chunks":["Du Fu, known as the “Sage of Poetry (诗圣)” is regarded as the greatest cel poet of realism in Chinese literature. Serving as a mirror of his times, his poems faithfully and profoundly reflect the social realities of the declining Tang Dynasty, functioning as poetic records of history."]},{"id":"u1_s9_p7","buf":["Du Fu perfected a sombre and meticulous literary style, and his works are considered the classics of Chinese realism. His poems, which span a wide range of styles, include masterpieces such as “On the Height” (《登高》) and “My Cottage Unroofed by Autumn Gales” (《茅屋为秋风所破歌》)."],"text":"Du Fu perfected a sombre and meticulous literary style, and his works are considered the classics of Chinese realism. His poems, which span a wide range of styles, include masterpieces such as “On the Height” (《登高》) and “My Cottage Unroofed by Autumn Gales” (《茅屋为秋风所破歌》).","chunks":["Du Fu perfected a sombre and meticulous literary style, and his works are considered the classics of Chinese realism. His poems, which span a wide range of styles, include masterpieces such as “On the Height” (《登高》) and “My Cottage Unroofed by Autumn Gales” (《茅屋为秋风所破歌》)."]},{"id":"u1_s9_p8","buf":["Other poets in this period can be grouped into two categories: frontier poets (边塞诗人), represented by Gao Shi and Cen Shen, and pastoral poets (田园诗人), represented by Meng Haoran and Wang Wei."],"text":"Other poets in this period can be grouped into two categories: frontier poets (边塞诗人), represented by Gao Shi and Cen Shen, and pastoral poets (田园诗人), represented by Meng Haoran and Wang Wei.","chunks":["Other poets in this period can be grouped into two categories: frontier poets (边塞诗人), represented by Gao Shi and Cen Shen, and pastoral poets (田园诗人), represented by Meng Haoran and Wang Wei."]},{"id":"u1_s9_p9","buf":["These two categories reflect the alternative paths available to scholars at the time: either serving the country through military or official posts, or choosing a life of seclusion. Frontier poems depict the scenery of the borders, military life, soldiers’ homesickness, passionate patriotism, and devotion to the state. Pastoral poets, following and developing the tradition of Tao Yuanming, depict rural life and celebrate the beauty of nature. In many of their poems, sentiments and nature are skillfully intertwined."],"text":"These two categories reflect the alternative paths available to scholars at the time: either serving the country through military or official posts, or choosing a life of seclusion. Frontier poems depict the scenery of the borders, military life, soldiers’ homesickness, passionate patriotism, and devotion to the state. Pastoral poets, following and developing the tradition of Tao Yuanming, depict rural life and celebrate the beauty of nature. In many of their poems, sentiments and nature are skillfully intertwined.","chunks":["These two categories reflect the alternative paths available to scholars at the time: either serving the country through military or official posts, or choosing a life of seclusion. Frontier poems depict the scenery of the borders, military life, soldiers’ homesickness, passionate patriotism, and devotion to the state. Pastoral poets, following and developing the tradition of Tao Yuanming, depict rural life and celebrate the beauty of nature. In many of their poems, sentiments and nature are skillfully intertwined."]},{"id":"u1_s9_p10","buf":["During the mid-Tang period, numerous poets produced exceptional works that, from various perspectives, reflected the social crises and hardships faced by ordinary people during the decline of the Tang Dynasty. The outstanding poet Bai Juyi followed Du Fu’s tradition and wrote poems that depicted social realities. His poetic treasures include satirical (讽刺的) and allegorical (讽喻的) poems. For example, “The Old Charcoal Seller” (《卖炭翁》) is a lament against official oppression. His most popular poems are the long narrative poems “Song of Everlasting Regret” (《长恨歌》) and “Song of a Pipa Player” (《琵琶行》)."],"text":"During the mid-Tang period, numerous poets produced exceptional works that, from various perspectives, reflected the social crises and hardships faced by ordinary people during the decline of the Tang Dynasty. The outstanding poet Bai Juyi followed Du Fu’s tradition and wrote poems that depicted social realities. His poetic treasures include satirical (讽刺的) and allegorical (讽喻的) poems. For example, “The Old Charcoal Seller” (《卖炭翁》) is a lament against official oppression. His most popular poems are the long narrative poems “Song of Everlasting Regret” (《长恨歌》) and “Song of a Pipa Player” (《琵琶行》).","chunks":["During the mid-Tang period, numerous poets produced exceptional works that, from various perspectives, reflected the social crises and hardships faced by ordinary people during the decline of the Tang Dynasty. The outstanding poet Bai Juyi followed Du Fu’s tradition and wrote poems that depicted social realities. His poetic treasures include satirical (讽刺的) and allegorical (讽喻的) poems. For example, “The Old Charcoal Seller” (《卖炭翁》) is a lament against official oppression. His most popular poems are the long narrative poems “Song of Everlasting Regret” (《长恨歌》) and “Song of a Pipa Player” (《琵琶行》)."]},{"id":"u1_s9_p11","buf":["With the deterioration of the government in the late Tang period, more and more poems reflected the hopelessness and helplessness felt by the people. Poets turned to themes of nostalgia for the splendour (辉煌) of bygone days. Among the most talented poets of this era were Li Shangyin and Du Mu. Some of their works focus on beauty, while others express a deep sense of disillusionment, often stemming from frustration at being unable to fully realise their talents due to limited opportunities."],"text":"With the deterioration of the government in the late Tang period, more and more poems reflected the hopelessness and helplessness felt by the people. Poets turned to themes of nostalgia for the splendour (辉煌) of bygone days. Among the most talented poets of this era were Li Shangyin and Du Mu. Some of their works focus on beauty, while others express a deep sense of disillusionment, often stemming from frustration at being unable to fully realise their talents due to limited opportunities.","chunks":["With the deterioration of the government in the late Tang period, more and more poems reflected the hopelessness and helplessness felt by the people. Poets turned to themes of nostalgia for the splendour (辉煌) of bygone days. Among the most talented poets of this era were Li Shangyin and Du Mu. Some of their works focus on beauty, while others express a deep sense of disillusionment, often stemming from frustration at being unable to fully realise their talents due to limited opportunities."]}]},{"id":"u1_s10","title":"宋词","paras":[{"id":"u1_s10_p1","buf":["The Song Dynasty is known for Song ci. Unlike regulated poetry, Song ci can express more refined and delicate feelings with irregular metre."],"text":"The Song Dynasty is known for Song ci. Unlike regulated poetry, Song ci can express more refined and delicate feelings with irregular metre.","chunks":["The Song Dynasty is known for Song ci. Unlike regulated poetry, Song ci can express more refined and delicate feelings with irregular metre."]},{"id":"u1_s10_p2","buf":["Ci originated in the Tang and the Five dynasties, and developed to maturity as a new literary form in the Song Dynasty. Evolving from poetry, it is characterised by being set to music and sung. Each piece of ci is written to a specific tune pattern, known as cipai, which determines its rhythm and structure. The “Beautiful Lady Yu (《虞美人》)”, “The Moon on the West River (西江月)”“Butterflies in Love with Flowers (《蝶恋花》) and “Immortals at the Magpie Bridge (《鹊桥仙》)” are all notable examples of cipai."],"text":"Ci originated in the Tang and the Five dynasties, and developed to maturity as a new literary form in the Song Dynasty. Evolving from poetry, it is characterised by being set to music and sung. Each piece of ci is written to a specific tune pattern, known as cipai, which determines its rhythm and structure. The “Beautiful Lady Yu (《虞美人》)”, “The Moon on the West River (西江月)”“Butterflies in Love with Flowers (《蝶恋花》) and “Immortals at the Magpie Bridge (《鹊桥仙》)” are all notable examples of cipai.","chunks":["Ci originated in the Tang and the Five dynasties, and developed to maturity as a new literary form in the Song Dynasty. Evolving from poetry, it is characterised by being set to music and sung. Each piece of ci is written to a specific tune pattern, known as cipai, which determines its rhythm and structure. The “Beautiful Lady Yu (《虞美人》)”, “The Moon on the West River (西江月)”“Butterflies in Love with Flowers (《蝶恋花》) and “Immortals at the Magpie Bridge (《鹊桥仙》)” are all notable examples of cipai."]},{"id":"u1_s10_p3","buf":["Song ci can be divided into three categories in terms of length: short lyrics (小令), medium lyrics (中调), and long lyrics (长调). In terms of structure, ci are usually divided into two stanzas, known as que (阕) or pian (片). Occasionally, they may consist of three or four stanzas, or just one. In terms of style, ci are traditionally categorised into two schools: the graceful and restrained school (婉约派) and the bold and unconstrained school (豪放派). The works of the former school are delicate and sentimental, often focusing on themes of family life and love, while those of the latter school are bold and expansive, often addressing major social issues like the fate of the nation."],"text":"Song ci can be divided into three categories in terms of length: short lyrics (小令), medium lyrics (中调), and long lyrics (长调). In terms of structure, ci are usually divided into two stanzas, known as que (阕) or pian (片). Occasionally, they may consist of three or four stanzas, or just one. In terms of style, ci are traditionally categorised into two schools: the graceful and restrained school (婉约派) and the bold and unconstrained school (豪放派). The works of the former school are delicate and sentimental, often focusing on themes of family life and love, while those of the latter school are bold and expansive, often addressing major social issues like the fate of the nation.","chunks":["Song ci can be divided into three categories in terms of length: short lyrics (小令), medium lyrics (中调), and long lyrics (长调). In terms of structure, ci are usually divided into two stanzas, known as que (阕) or pian (片). Occasionally, they may consist of three or four stanzas, or just one. In terms of style, ci are traditionally categorised into two schools: the graceful and restrained school (婉约派) and the bold and unconstrained school (豪放派). The works of the former school are delicate and sentimental, often focusing on themes of family life and love, while those of the latter school are bold and expansive, often addressing major social issues like the fate of the nation."]},{"id":"u1_s10_p4","buf":["The graceful and restrained school is represented by Liu Yong, Li Qingzhao, Yan Shu, and Ouyang Xiu. Most of their ci focus on themes of love and parting, reflecting the tastes of elite literati (文人学士) and the sophisticated, leisurely lifestyle of the aristocracy. Liu Yong is acknowledged as the first master of this school. Li Qingzhao is regarded as the most talented female representative of the school. She expressed her emotions in simple and plain language. Her style is original and natural, with lyrics that are genuine and touching. Her famous ci “Slow, Slow Song” (《声声慢》) conveys her intense grief and loneliness during a time of personal and national turmoil."],"text":"The graceful and restrained school is represented by Liu Yong, Li Qingzhao, Yan Shu, and Ouyang Xiu. Most of their ci focus on themes of love and parting, reflecting the tastes of elite literati (文人学士) and the sophisticated, leisurely lifestyle of the aristocracy. Liu Yong is acknowledged as the first master of this school. Li Qingzhao is regarded as the most talented female representative of the school. She expressed her emotions in simple and plain language. Her style is original and natural, with lyrics that are genuine and touching. Her famous ci “Slow, Slow Song” (《声声慢》) conveys her intense grief and loneliness during a time of personal and national turmoil.","chunks":["The graceful and restrained school is represented by Liu Yong, Li Qingzhao, Yan Shu, and Ouyang Xiu. Most of their ci focus on themes of love and parting, reflecting the tastes of elite literati (文人学士) and the sophisticated, leisurely lifestyle of the aristocracy. Liu Yong is acknowledged as the first master of this school. Li Qingzhao is regarded as the most talented female representative of the school. She expressed her emotions in simple and plain language. Her style is original and natural, with lyrics that are genuine and touching. Her famous ci “Slow, Slow Song” (《声声慢》) conveys her intense grief and loneliness during a time of personal and national turmoil."]},{"id":"u1_s10_p5","buf":["The bold and unconstrained school is represented by Su Shi, Xin Qiji, and Lu You. With his extraordinarily vigorous style, Su Shi brought innovations to ci and helped broaden its scope, elevate its status, and establish the school. Some of his most famous ci include “Memories of the Past at Red Cliff (to the tune of ‘Charm of a Maiden Singer’)” (《念奴娇·赤壁怀古》), “The Mid-Autumn Festival (to the tune of ‘Prelude to Water Melody )”(《水调歌头·明月几时有》), and “Dreaming of My Deceased Wife (to the tune of ‘A Riverside Town’)” (《江城子·乙卯正月二十日夜记梦》). Xin Qiji, another remarkable ci poet, elevated the ci of the bold and unconstrained school to new heights by enriching its content and improving its form. His representative works include “On the Riverside Tower at Jiankang (to the tune of “Water Dragon Chant’)” (《水龙吟·登建康赏心亭》) and “Thinking of Ancient Heroes in the Tower on the Northern Hill at Jingkou (to the tune of ‘Always Having Fun’)” (《永遇乐·京口北固亭怀古》)."],"text":"The bold and unconstrained school is represented by Su Shi, Xin Qiji, and Lu You. With his extraordinarily vigorous style, Su Shi brought innovations to ci and helped broaden its scope, elevate its status, and establish the school. Some of his most famous ci include “Memories of the Past at Red Cliff (to the tune of ‘Charm of a Maiden Singer’)” (《念奴娇·赤壁怀古》), “The Mid-Autumn Festival (to the tune of ‘Prelude to Water Melody )”(《水调歌头·明月几时有》), and “Dreaming of My Deceased Wife (to the tune of ‘A Riverside Town’)” (《江城子·乙卯正月二十日夜记梦》). Xin Qiji, another remarkable ci poet, elevated the ci of the bold and unconstrained school to new heights by enriching its content and improving its form. His representative works include “On the Riverside Tower at Jiankang (to the tune of “Water Dragon Chant’)” (《水龙吟·登建康赏心亭》) and “Thinking of Ancient Heroes in the Tower on the Northern Hill at Jingkou (to the tune of ‘Always Having Fun’)” (《永遇乐·京口北固亭怀古》).","chunks":["The bold and unconstrained school is represented by Su Shi, Xin Qiji, and Lu You. With his extraordinarily vigorous style, Su Shi brought innovations to ci and helped broaden its scope, elevate its status, and establish the school. Some of his most famous ci include “Memories of the Past at Red Cliff (to the tune of ‘Charm of a Maiden Singer’)” (《念奴娇·赤壁怀古》), “The Mid-Autumn Festival (to the tune of ‘Prelude to Water Melody )”(《水调歌头·明月几时有》), and “Dreaming of My Deceased Wife (to the tune of ‘A Riverside Town’)” (《江城子·乙卯正月二十日夜记梦》). Xin Qiji, another remarkable ci poet, elevated the ci of the bold and unconstrained school to new heights by enriching its content and improving its form. His representative works include “On the Riverside Tower at Jiankang (to the tune of “Water Dragon Chant’)” (《水龙吟·登建康赏心亭》) and “Thinking of Ancient Heroes in the Tower on the Northern Hill at Jingkou (to the tune of ‘Always Having Fun’)” (《永遇乐·京口北固亭怀古》)."]}]},{"id":"u1_s11","title":"元曲","paras":[{"id":"u1_s11_p1","buf":["Qu (曲) is a literary form that emerged later than poetry and ci. It generally refers to the northern and southern melodic forms created during the Song and Jin dynasties. Since qu reached its peak in the Yuan Dynasty, it is commonly known as Yuan qu or Yuan opera. Qu is similar to ci in form but is more flexible in sentence structure and often incorporates colloquial language. The two main types of qu are xiqu (戏曲), also known as juqu (剧曲), and sanqu (散曲)."],"text":"Qu (曲) is a literary form that emerged later than poetry and ci. It generally refers to the northern and southern melodic forms created during the Song and Jin dynasties. Since qu reached its peak in the Yuan Dynasty, it is commonly known as Yuan qu or Yuan opera. Qu is similar to ci in form but is more flexible in sentence structure and often incorporates colloquial language. The two main types of qu are xiqu (戏曲), also known as juqu (剧曲), and sanqu (散曲).","chunks":["Qu (曲) is a literary form that emerged later than poetry and ci. It generally refers to the northern and southern melodic forms created during the Song and Jin dynasties. Since qu reached its peak in the Yuan Dynasty, it is commonly known as Yuan qu or Yuan opera. Qu is similar to ci in form but is more flexible in sentence structure and often incorporates colloquial language. The two main types of qu are xiqu (戏曲), also known as juqu (剧曲), and sanqu (散曲)."]},{"id":"u1_s11_p2","buf":["An important part of xiqu is Yuan zaju (杂剧), which originated from the northern zaju of the Song Dynasty. It consists of four acts (折) and occasionally a prologue (楔子) before the first act or an interlude between acts. Each act features northern opera melodies that match the same tune and rhyme of the spoken parts."],"text":"An important part of xiqu is Yuan zaju (杂剧), which originated from the northern zaju of the Song Dynasty. It consists of four acts (折) and occasionally a prologue (楔子) before the first act or an interlude between acts. Each act features northern opera melodies that match the same tune and rhyme of the spoken parts.","chunks":["An important part of xiqu is Yuan zaju (杂剧), which originated from the northern zaju of the Song Dynasty. It consists of four acts (折) and occasionally a prologue (楔子) before the first act or an interlude between acts. Each act features northern opera melodies that match the same tune and rhyme of the spoken parts."]},{"id":"u1_s11_p3","buf":["Important zaju writers include Guan Hanqing and Bai Pu. Guan Hanging, the most highly-reputed dramatist of the period, wrote the masterpiece tragedy “The Injustice to Dou E” (《窦娥冤》). It is about the young widow, Dou E, who is falsely accused and then finally vindicated. Through this work, Guan poignantly (深刻地) exposed the social injustice of his time."],"text":"Important zaju writers include Guan Hanqing and Bai Pu. Guan Hanging, the most highly-reputed dramatist of the period, wrote the masterpiece tragedy “The Injustice to Dou E” (《窦娥冤》). It is about the young widow, Dou E, who is falsely accused and then finally vindicated. Through this work, Guan poignantly (深刻地) exposed the social injustice of his time.","chunks":["Important zaju writers include Guan Hanqing and Bai Pu. Guan Hanging, the most highly-reputed dramatist of the period, wrote the masterpiece tragedy “The Injustice to Dou E” (《窦娥冤》). It is about the young widow, Dou E, who is falsely accused and then finally vindicated. Through this work, Guan poignantly (深刻地) exposed the social injustice of his time."]},{"id":"u1_s11_p4","buf":["Like other forms of poetry, sanqu describes a scene, sentiment, or event and can be sung. However, it does not include spoken dialogue or stage instructions for performers. The forms of sanqu include xiaoling (小令 : short tonal poem), santao (散套: a sequence of sanqu songs of a certain musical mode), and daiguo qu (带过曲 : a small-scale suite composed of two or three musical sections). Ma Zhiyuan’s “Autumn Thoughts (to the tune of ‘Heavenly Pure Sand’)” (《天净沙·秋思》), a xiaoling, enjoys great popularity. Ma’s work of 28 characters depicts the wilderness of a strange land and expresses his loneliness and sadness."],"text":"Like other forms of poetry, sanqu describes a scene, sentiment, or event and can be sung. However, it does not include spoken dialogue or stage instructions for performers. The forms of sanqu include xiaoling (小令 : short tonal poem), santao (散套: a sequence of sanqu songs of a certain musical mode), and daiguo qu (带过曲 : a small-scale suite composed of two or three musical sections). Ma Zhiyuan’s “Autumn Thoughts (to the tune of ‘Heavenly Pure Sand’)” (《天净沙·秋思》), a xiaoling, enjoys great popularity. Ma’s work of 28 characters depicts the wilderness of a strange land and expresses his loneliness and sadness.","chunks":["Like other forms of poetry, sanqu describes a scene, sentiment, or event and can be sung. However, it does not include spoken dialogue or stage instructions for performers. The forms of sanqu include xiaoling (小令 : short tonal poem), santao (散套: a sequence of sanqu songs of a certain musical mode), and daiguo qu (带过曲 : a small-scale suite composed of two or three musical sections). Ma Zhiyuan’s “Autumn Thoughts (to the tune of ‘Heavenly Pure Sand’)” (《天净沙·秋思》), a xiaoling, enjoys great popularity. Ma’s work of 28 characters depicts the wilderness of a strange land and expresses his loneliness and sadness."]}]},{"id":"u1_s12","title":"明清小说","paras":[{"id":"u1_s12_p1","buf":["In the Song Dynasty, huaben (话本), written in colloquial language, became popular. By the Ming and Qing dynasties, novels flourished as a lively and free literary form. The language of these novels was simple, easy to understand, and welcomed by the public."],"text":"In the Song Dynasty, huaben (话本), written in colloquial language, became popular. By the Ming and Qing dynasties, novels flourished as a lively and free literary form. The language of these novels was simple, easy to understand, and welcomed by the public.","chunks":["In the Song Dynasty, huaben (话本), written in colloquial language, became popular. By the Ming and Qing dynasties, novels flourished as a lively and free literary form. The language of these novels was simple, easy to understand, and welcomed by the public."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p2","buf":["The Ming and Qing dynasties saw the publication of the Four Great Classical Novels: Romance of the Three Kingdoms (《三国演义》), Water Margin (《水浒传》), Journey to the West (《西游记》), and A Dream of Red Mansions (《红楼梦》). They have been celebrated for centuries for their rich historical and cultural significance."],"text":"The Ming and Qing dynasties saw the publication of the Four Great Classical Novels: Romance of the Three Kingdoms (《三国演义》), Water Margin (《水浒传》), Journey to the West (《西游记》), and A Dream of Red Mansions (《红楼梦》). They have been celebrated for centuries for their rich historical and cultural significance.","chunks":["The Ming and Qing dynasties saw the publication of the Four Great Classical Novels: Romance of the Three Kingdoms (《三国演义》), Water Margin (《水浒传》), Journey to the West (《西游记》), and A Dream of Red Mansions (《红楼梦》). They have been celebrated for centuries for their rich historical and cultural significance."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p3","buf":["Romance of the Three Kingdoms by Luo Guanzhong is recognised as the earliest historical novel written in chapters in China. Based on history books and folk legends, the novel describes the rise and fall of the three kingdoms of Wei, Shu, and Wu, and the complex political, military, and diplomatic struggles during that period. Romance of the Three Kingdoms vividly expresses the people’s hatred towards corrupt rulers and their longing for virtuous leaders, loyal friends, and an ideal political order."],"text":"Romance of the Three Kingdoms by Luo Guanzhong is recognised as the earliest historical novel written in chapters in China. Based on history books and folk legends, the novel describes the rise and fall of the three kingdoms of Wei, Shu, and Wu, and the complex political, military, and diplomatic struggles during that period. Romance of the Three Kingdoms vividly expresses the people’s hatred towards corrupt rulers and their longing for virtuous leaders, loyal friends, and an ideal political order.","chunks":["Romance of the Three Kingdoms by Luo Guanzhong is recognised as the earliest historical novel written in chapters in China. Based on history books and folk legends, the novel describes the rise and fall of the three kingdoms of Wei, Shu, and Wu, and the complex political, military, and diplomatic struggles during that period. Romance of the Three Kingdoms vividly expresses the people’s hatred towards corrupt rulers and their longing for virtuous leaders, loyal friends, and an ideal political order."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p4","buf":["The basic literary style of Romance of the Three Kingdoms is realism, but the plot arrangement and the portrayal of historical figures are imbued with romantic elements. The novel is written in clear, concise, and vivid language. Dialogue plays an important role in revealing the characters’ personalities. Liu Bei is portrayed as an ideal ruler who adheres to benevolent policies (仁政). Zhuge Liang is depicted as an outstanding statesman and strategist, whose foresight is godlike. His name has become synonymous with inexhaustible resourcefulness. Zhang Fei’s bravery and straightforwardness, Guan Yu's loyalty, and Cao Cao’s cunning are also skillfully depicted."],"text":"The basic literary style of Romance of the Three Kingdoms is realism, but the plot arrangement and the portrayal of historical figures are imbued with romantic elements. The novel is written in clear, concise, and vivid language. Dialogue plays an important role in revealing the characters’ personalities. Liu Bei is portrayed as an ideal ruler who adheres to benevolent policies (仁政). Zhuge Liang is depicted as an outstanding statesman and strategist, whose foresight is godlike. His name has become synonymous with inexhaustible resourcefulness. Zhang Fei’s bravery and straightforwardness, Guan Yu's loyalty, and Cao Cao’s cunning are also skillfully depicted.","chunks":["The basic literary style of Romance of the Three Kingdoms is realism, but the plot arrangement and the portrayal of historical figures are imbued with romantic elements. The novel is written in clear, concise, and vivid language. Dialogue plays an important role in revealing the characters’ personalities. Liu Bei is portrayed as an ideal ruler who adheres to benevolent policies (仁政). Zhuge Liang is depicted as an outstanding statesman and strategist, whose foresight is godlike. His name has become synonymous with inexhaustible resourcefulness. Zhang Fei’s bravery and straightforwardness, Guan Yu's loyalty, and Cao Cao’s cunning are also skillfully depicted."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p5","buf":["Water Margin by Shi Nai’an tells the story of the rebellion of 108 heroes at the end of the Northern Song Dynasty. It is the most influential novel in Chinese literature, dealing with revolts by the oppressed. The novel provides a comprehensive depiction of a real uprising by the oppressed, tracing its development and failure, and uncovering its social roots as well as the underlying reasons for its downfall. One of the book’s most prominent artistic achievements is its introduction of many of the most iconic characters in classical Chinese fiction."],"text":"Water Margin by Shi Nai’an tells the story of the rebellion of 108 heroes at the end of the Northern Song Dynasty. It is the most influential novel in Chinese literature, dealing with revolts by the oppressed. The novel provides a comprehensive depiction of a real uprising by the oppressed, tracing its development and failure, and uncovering its social roots as well as the underlying reasons for its downfall. One of the book’s most prominent artistic achievements is its introduction of many of the most iconic characters in classical Chinese fiction.","chunks":["Water Margin by Shi Nai’an tells the story of the rebellion of 108 heroes at the end of the Northern Song Dynasty. It is the most influential novel in Chinese literature, dealing with revolts by the oppressed. The novel provides a comprehensive depiction of a real uprising by the oppressed, tracing its development and failure, and uncovering its social roots as well as the underlying reasons for its downfall. One of the book’s most prominent artistic achievements is its introduction of many of the most iconic characters in classical Chinese fiction."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p6","buf":["Shi Nai an was skilled at revealing the inner world of characters through their behaviour and language. His narrative techniques were influenced by traditional storytelling methods. He also excelled at portraying each character's appearance and personality. By describing their different life experiences, he revealed the characters’ dispositions and their resistance to oppression."],"text":"Shi Nai an was skilled at revealing the inner world of characters through their behaviour and language. His narrative techniques were influenced by traditional storytelling methods. He also excelled at portraying each character's appearance and personality. By describing their different life experiences, he revealed the characters’ dispositions and their resistance to oppression.","chunks":["Shi Nai an was skilled at revealing the inner world of characters through their behaviour and language. His narrative techniques were influenced by traditional storytelling methods. He also excelled at portraying each character's appearance and personality. By describing their different life experiences, he revealed the characters’ dispositions and their resistance to oppression."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p7","buf":["Journey to the West by Wu Cheng’en is an outstanding romantic work filled with fantastical tales. It is widely recognised as a masterpiece about deities (神仙) and spirits. The book recounts the epic tale of Tang Sanzang, a Buddhist monk, and his three disciples—Sun Wukong (the Monkey King), Zhu Bajie (Pigsy), and Sha Wujing (Monk Sha)— as they embark on a perilous journey to retrieve the sacred Buddhist scriptures. Along the way, they face numerous challenges, battle demons and monsters, and endure 81 tribulations (苦难)."],"text":"Journey to the West by Wu Cheng’en is an outstanding romantic work filled with fantastical tales. It is widely recognised as a masterpiece about deities (神仙) and spirits. The book recounts the epic tale of Tang Sanzang, a Buddhist monk, and his three disciples—Sun Wukong (the Monkey King), Zhu Bajie (Pigsy), and Sha Wujing (Monk Sha)— as they embark on a perilous journey to retrieve the sacred Buddhist scriptures. Along the way, they face numerous challenges, battle demons and monsters, and endure 81 tribulations (苦难).","chunks":["Journey to the West by Wu Cheng’en is an outstanding romantic work filled with fantastical tales. It is widely recognised as a masterpiece about deities (神仙) and spirits. The book recounts the epic tale of Tang Sanzang, a Buddhist monk, and his three disciples—Sun Wukong (the Monkey King), Zhu Bajie (Pigsy), and Sha Wujing (Monk Sha)— as they embark on a perilous journey to retrieve the sacred Buddhist scriptures. Along the way, they face numerous challenges, battle demons and monsters, and endure 81 tribulations (苦难)."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p8","buf":["Wu Cheng’en illuminated the intelligence, bravery, and loyalty of Sun Wukong, the piety of Tang Sanzang, the humour, good nature, and flaws of Zhu Bajie, and the kindness of Sha Wujing. Some fantastic and thrilling tales from the novel are still popular among readers today: “The Monkey King Wreaks Havoc in Heaven” (《大闹天宫》), “The Three Battles with the White-Boned Demon” (《三打白骨精》), and “The Three Borrowings of the Palm-Leaf Fan” (《三借芭蕉扇》). Through tales of deities and spirits, Wu Cheng’en exposed the darkness and corruption of feudal society, criticised social realities, and gave voice to the hopes and aspirations of the people for justice, equality, and a better world."],"text":"Wu Cheng’en illuminated the intelligence, bravery, and loyalty of Sun Wukong, the piety of Tang Sanzang, the humour, good nature, and flaws of Zhu Bajie, and the kindness of Sha Wujing. Some fantastic and thrilling tales from the novel are still popular among readers today: “The Monkey King Wreaks Havoc in Heaven” (《大闹天宫》), “The Three Battles with the White-Boned Demon” (《三打白骨精》), and “The Three Borrowings of the Palm-Leaf Fan” (《三借芭蕉扇》). Through tales of deities and spirits, Wu Cheng’en exposed the darkness and corruption of feudal society, criticised social realities, and gave voice to the hopes and aspirations of the people for justice, equality, and a better world.","chunks":["Wu Cheng’en illuminated the intelligence, bravery, and loyalty of Sun Wukong, the piety of Tang Sanzang, the humour, good nature, and flaws of Zhu Bajie, and the kindness of Sha Wujing. Some fantastic and thrilling tales from the novel are still popular among readers today: “The Monkey King Wreaks Havoc in Heaven” (《大闹天宫》), “The Three Battles with the White-Boned Demon” (《三打白骨精》), and “The Three Borrowings of the Palm-Leaf Fan” (《三借芭蕉扇》). Through tales of deities and spirits, Wu Cheng’en exposed the darkness and corruption of feudal society, criticised social realities, and gave voice to the hopes and aspirations of the people for justice, equality, and a better world."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p9","buf":["A Dream of Red Mansions represents the pinnacle (顶峰) of classical Chinese novels. It has long been acknowledged as one of the greatest works in Chinese literature. Cao Xueqin is generally credited as the author of the first 80 chapters. This novel vividly portrays the rise and fall of a large feudal family. The central thread of the novel is the tragic love story between Jia Baoyu and Lin Daiyu. Rather than just narrating the love story, it explores the social origins of the tragedy through probing (探究) the characters’ inner worlds and their complex relationships. The characters Jia Baoyu, Lin Daiyu, Xue Baochai, Wang Xifeng, and Granny Liu have become enduring artistic figures of lasting appeal."],"text":"A Dream of Red Mansions represents the pinnacle (顶峰) of classical Chinese novels. It has long been acknowledged as one of the greatest works in Chinese literature. Cao Xueqin is generally credited as the author of the first 80 chapters. This novel vividly portrays the rise and fall of a large feudal family. The central thread of the novel is the tragic love story between Jia Baoyu and Lin Daiyu. Rather than just narrating the love story, it explores the social origins of the tragedy through probing (探究) the characters’ inner worlds and their complex relationships. The characters Jia Baoyu, Lin Daiyu, Xue Baochai, Wang Xifeng, and Granny Liu have become enduring artistic figures of lasting appeal.","chunks":["A Dream of Red Mansions represents the pinnacle (顶峰) of classical Chinese novels. It has long been acknowledged as one of the greatest works in Chinese literature. Cao Xueqin is generally credited as the author of the first 80 chapters. This novel vividly portrays the rise and fall of a large feudal family. The central thread of the novel is the tragic love story between Jia Baoyu and Lin Daiyu. Rather than just narrating the love story, it explores the social origins of the tragedy through probing (探究) the characters’ inner worlds and their complex relationships. The characters Jia Baoyu, Lin Daiyu, Xue Baochai, Wang Xifeng, and Granny Liu have become enduring artistic figures of lasting appeal."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p10","buf":["The plot of the novel is ingeniously (4A 心地) arranged. Its narratives use mature colloquial language, plain but elegant, explicit but expressive. The novel contains poems, ci, lyric verses, and prose essays, which fit the plot and reflect the fates of the characters. The novel is truly a panorama (全景画) of feudal society and is regarded as an encyclopedia (百科全书) of Chinese culture."],"text":"The plot of the novel is ingeniously (4A 心地) arranged. Its narratives use mature colloquial language, plain but elegant, explicit but expressive. The novel contains poems, ci, lyric verses, and prose essays, which fit the plot and reflect the fates of the characters. The novel is truly a panorama (全景画) of feudal society and is regarded as an encyclopedia (百科全书) of Chinese culture.","chunks":["The plot of the novel is ingeniously (4A 心地) arranged. Its narratives use mature colloquial language, plain but elegant, explicit but expressive. The novel contains poems, ci, lyric verses, and prose essays, which fit the plot and reflect the fates of the characters. The novel is truly a panorama (全景画) of feudal society and is regarded as an encyclopedia (百科全书) of Chinese culture."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p11","buf":["Besides the Four Great Classical Novels, the “Three Words (三言)” by Feng Menglong and the “Two Slaps (二拍)” by Ling Mengchu are two outstanding collections of short stories from the Ming Dynasty. The term “Three Words” refers to Clear Words to Illustrate the World, Ordinary Words to Warn the World, and Lasting Words to Awaken the World. The term “Two Slaps” refers to Slapping the Table in Amazement I and II. These works are regarded as exemplary illustrations of Chinese feudal society, containing many descriptions of the daily life of townspeople, and are classified as “worldly novels (世情小说)”."],"text":"Besides the Four Great Classical Novels, the “Three Words (三言)” by Feng Menglong and the “Two Slaps (二拍)” by Ling Mengchu are two outstanding collections of short stories from the Ming Dynasty. The term “Three Words” refers to Clear Words to Illustrate the World, Ordinary Words to Warn the World, and Lasting Words to Awaken the World. The term “Two Slaps” refers to Slapping the Table in Amazement I and II. These works are regarded as exemplary illustrations of Chinese feudal society, containing many descriptions of the daily life of townspeople, and are classified as “worldly novels (世情小说)”.","chunks":["Besides the Four Great Classical Novels, the “Three Words (三言)” by Feng Menglong and the “Two Slaps (二拍)” by Ling Mengchu are two outstanding collections of short stories from the Ming Dynasty. The term “Three Words” refers to Clear Words to Illustrate the World, Ordinary Words to Warn the World, and Lasting Words to Awaken the World. The term “Two Slaps” refers to Slapping the Table in Amazement I and II. These works are regarded as exemplary illustrations of Chinese feudal society, containing many descriptions of the daily life of townspeople, and are classified as “worldly novels (世情小说)”."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p12","buf":["The most widely-read collection of short stories in the Qing Dynasty is Strange Tales from the Liaozhai Studio (《聊斋志异》)by Pu Songling, which portrays a fascinating array of flower-spirits, fox-fairies, and ghosts. It inherits the traditions of the supernatural tales in the Wei and Jin dynasties and the legendary tales (传奇小说) in the Tang Dynasty. Blending romanticism and realism, it implies a serious criticism of social problems. Pu Songling’s characters are well-rounded and adeptly portrayed. The language is fluent and vivid; the plot is intricate; and the structure is cohesive."],"text":"The most widely-read collection of short stories in the Qing Dynasty is Strange Tales from the Liaozhai Studio (《聊斋志异》)by Pu Songling, which portrays a fascinating array of flower-spirits, fox-fairies, and ghosts. It inherits the traditions of the supernatural tales in the Wei and Jin dynasties and the legendary tales (传奇小说) in the Tang Dynasty. Blending romanticism and realism, it implies a serious criticism of social problems. Pu Songling’s characters are well-rounded and adeptly portrayed. The language is fluent and vivid; the plot is intricate; and the structure is cohesive.","chunks":["The most widely-read collection of short stories in the Qing Dynasty is Strange Tales from the Liaozhai Studio (《聊斋志异》)by Pu Songling, which portrays a fascinating array of flower-spirits, fox-fairies, and ghosts. It inherits the traditions of the supernatural tales in the Wei and Jin dynasties and the legendary tales (传奇小说) in the Tang Dynasty. Blending romanticism and realism, it implies a serious criticism of social problems. Pu Songling’s characters are well-rounded and adeptly portrayed. The language is fluent and vivid; the plot is intricate; and the structure is cohesive."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p13","buf":["The Scholars (《儒林外史》), authored by Wu Jingzi is alandmark colloquial and satirical novel in Chinese literature."],"text":"The Scholars (《儒林外史》), authored by Wu Jingzi is alandmark colloquial and satirical novel in Chinese literature.","chunks":["The Scholars (《儒林外史》), authored by Wu Jingzi is alandmark colloquial and satirical novel in Chinese literature."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p14","buf":["For the first time in literary history, the novel uses the literati as its primary subject and material, creating an artistic world that is deeply rooted in the realities of a specific historical era. It portrays a group of feudal scholars and criticises the eight-part essay (八股文) and the imperial civil examination system (科举制度). Its 56 chapters consist of a series of separate episodes (情节) without a single hero throughout the narrative. The structure reflects internal harmony and balance, while Wu Jingzi’s character portrayal is simple yet insightful."],"text":"For the first time in literary history, the novel uses the literati as its primary subject and material, creating an artistic world that is deeply rooted in the realities of a specific historical era. It portrays a group of feudal scholars and criticises the eight-part essay (八股文) and the imperial civil examination system (科举制度). Its 56 chapters consist of a series of separate episodes (情节) without a single hero throughout the narrative. The structure reflects internal harmony and balance, while Wu Jingzi’s character portrayal is simple yet insightful.","chunks":["For the first time in literary history, the novel uses the literati as its primary subject and material, creating an artistic world that is deeply rooted in the realities of a specific historical era. It portrays a group of feudal scholars and criticises the eight-part essay (八股文) and the imperial civil examination system (科举制度). Its 56 chapters consist of a series of separate episodes (情节) without a single hero throughout the narrative. The structure reflects internal harmony and balance, while Wu Jingzi’s character portrayal is simple yet insightful."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p15","buf":["The Scholars is not only the first book to create a diverse array of images of scholars and intellectuals, but also the first to openly challenge the notion that academic pursuits should solely serve the purpose of obtaining official positions. It occupies an important place in the history of Chinese fiction and has greatly influenced later satirical novels."],"text":"The Scholars is not only the first book to create a diverse array of images of scholars and intellectuals, but also the first to openly challenge the notion that academic pursuits should solely serve the purpose of obtaining official positions. It occupies an important place in the history of Chinese fiction and has greatly influenced later satirical novels.","chunks":["The Scholars is not only the first book to create a diverse array of images of scholars and intellectuals, but also the first to openly challenge the notion that academic pursuits should solely serve the purpose of obtaining official positions. It occupies an important place in the history of Chinese fiction and has greatly influenced later satirical novels."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p16","buf":["Following the Hundred Days’ Reform (百日维新) in 1898, supporters called for revolutionary poems and novels to arouse people’s political awareness. Some writers produced novels denouncing the feudal system, which was the source of widespread dissatisfaction among the people. Many works voiced opposition to the foreign invasions and exposed the evils of the time."],"text":"Following the Hundred Days’ Reform (百日维新) in 1898, supporters called for revolutionary poems and novels to arouse people’s political awareness. Some writers produced novels denouncing the feudal system, which was the source of widespread dissatisfaction among the people. Many works voiced opposition to the foreign invasions and exposed the evils of the time.","chunks":["Following the Hundred Days’ Reform (百日维新) in 1898, supporters called for revolutionary poems and novels to arouse people’s political awareness. Some writers produced novels denouncing the feudal system, which was the source of widespread dissatisfaction among the people. Many works voiced opposition to the foreign invasions and exposed the evils of the time."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p17","buf":["Two of the best-known poets of this period are Huang Zunxian and Liu Yazi. Huang Zunxian’s poems reflect people’s thoughts and feelings, the impact of new intellectual trends, and the spiritual transformations occurring in Chinese society. Liu Yazi was one of the founders of the Southern Society (南社), China’s first modern revolutionary literary society, and his passionate poems are imbued with patriotism."],"text":"Two of the best-known poets of this period are Huang Zunxian and Liu Yazi. Huang Zunxian’s poems reflect people’s thoughts and feelings, the impact of new intellectual trends, and the spiritual transformations occurring in Chinese society. Liu Yazi was one of the founders of the Southern Society (南社), China’s first modern revolutionary literary society, and his passionate poems are imbued with patriotism.","chunks":["Two of the best-known poets of this period are Huang Zunxian and Liu Yazi. Huang Zunxian’s poems reflect people’s thoughts and feelings, the impact of new intellectual trends, and the spiritual transformations occurring in Chinese society. Liu Yazi was one of the founders of the Southern Society (南社), China’s first modern revolutionary literary society, and his passionate poems are imbued with patriotism."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p18","buf":["Influenced by the reform movement, writers in the late Qing Dynasty produced a significant number of new novels. Among the most famous works are Officialdom Unmasked by Li Boyuan (李伯元 ,《官场现形记》), Bizarre Happenings Eyewitnessed over Two Decades by Wu Jianren (吴趼人, 《二十年目睹之怪现状》), Travels of Lao Can by Liu E (刘鹗, 《老残游记》) and Flowers in the Sea of Evil by Zeng Pu (曾朴, 《孽海花》). These four novels, collectively known as the “Four Satirical Novels” in the late Qing Dynasty, vividly portray the decline of the empire and exemplify the profound social values embedded in fiction during this period."],"text":"Influenced by the reform movement, writers in the late Qing Dynasty produced a significant number of new novels. Among the most famous works are Officialdom Unmasked by Li Boyuan (李伯元 ,《官场现形记》), Bizarre Happenings Eyewitnessed over Two Decades by Wu Jianren (吴趼人, 《二十年目睹之怪现状》), Travels of Lao Can by Liu E (刘鹗, 《老残游记》) and Flowers in the Sea of Evil by Zeng Pu (曾朴, 《孽海花》). These four novels, collectively known as the “Four Satirical Novels” in the late Qing Dynasty, vividly portray the decline of the empire and exemplify the profound social values embedded in fiction during this period.","chunks":["Influenced by the reform movement, writers in the late Qing Dynasty produced a significant number of new novels. Among the most famous works are Officialdom Unmasked by Li Boyuan (李伯元 ,《官场现形记》), Bizarre Happenings Eyewitnessed over Two Decades by Wu Jianren (吴趼人, 《二十年目睹之怪现状》), Travels of Lao Can by Liu E (刘鹗, 《老残游记》) and Flowers in the Sea of Evil by Zeng Pu (曾朴, 《孽海花》). These four novels, collectively known as the “Four Satirical Novels” in the late Qing Dynasty, vividly portray the decline of the empire and exemplify the profound social values embedded in fiction during this period."]},{"id":"u1_s12_p19","buf":["Since the late Qing Dynasty, a wide range of books translated from Western languages have also emerged. The leading translators were Yan Fu and Lin Shu. The writers of the modern period were greatly influenced by European literature."],"text":"Since the late Qing Dynasty, a wide range of books translated from Western languages have also emerged. The leading translators were Yan Fu and Lin Shu. The writers of the modern period were greatly influenced by European literature.","chunks":["Since the late Qing Dynasty, a wide range of books translated from Western languages have also emerged. The leading translators were Yan Fu and Lin Shu. The writers of the modern period were greatly influenced by European literature."]}]}]},{"id":"u2","title":"现代文学","sections":[{"id":"u2_s1","title":"现代文学","paras":[{"id":"u2_s1_p1","buf":["The May Fourth Movement in 1919 marked the beginning of a new epoch (时代) in China. The main theme during the Chinese People’s War of Resistance Against Japanese Aggression was the search for survival and liberation. The founding of New China in 1949 serves as a signpost for the beginning of modern Chinese literature. Works of this period reflect the hardships and tremendous sacrifices during the War of Liberation from 1945 to 1949, while also eulogising the selflessness displayed in the building of socialist New China. Important representatives of modern Chinese literature include Lu Xun, Guo Moruo, Mao Dun, and Ba Jin."],"text":"The May Fourth Movement in 1919 marked the beginning of a new epoch (时代) in China. The main theme during the Chinese People’s War of Resistance Against Japanese Aggression was the search for survival and liberation. The founding of New China in 1949 serves as a signpost for the beginning of modern Chinese literature. Works of this period reflect the hardships and tremendous sacrifices during the War of Liberation from 1945 to 1949, while also eulogising the selflessness displayed in the building of socialist New China. Important representatives of modern Chinese literature include Lu Xun, Guo Moruo, Mao Dun, and Ba Jin.","chunks":["The May Fourth Movement in 1919 marked the beginning of a new epoch (时代) in China. The main theme during the Chinese People’s War of Resistance Against Japanese Aggression was the search for survival and liberation. The founding of New China in 1949 serves as a signpost for the beginning of modern Chinese literature. Works of this period reflect the hardships and tremendous sacrifices during the War of Liberation from 1945 to 1949, while also eulogising the selflessness displayed in the building of socialist New China. Important representatives of modern Chinese literature include Lu Xun, Guo Moruo, Mao Dun, and Ba Jin."]}]},{"id":"u2_s2","title":"鲁迅（1881—1936）","paras":[{"id":"u2_s2_p1","buf":["Born in 1881 in Shaoxing, Zhejiang Province, Lu Xun, also known as Zhou Shuren, was a great thinker and a revolutionary figure in literature. He was one of the founders of modern Chinese literature, using literature and art as weapons to transform the national spirit and character. His line, “Fierce-browed, I coolly defy a thousand pointing fingers; Head-bowed, like a willing ox I serve the children (横眉冷对千夫指，俯首甘为巴子牛)” vividly portrays his personality."],"text":"Born in 1881 in Shaoxing, Zhejiang Province, Lu Xun, also known as Zhou Shuren, was a great thinker and a revolutionary figure in literature. He was one of the founders of modern Chinese literature, using literature and art as weapons to transform the national spirit and character. His line, “Fierce-browed, I coolly defy a thousand pointing fingers; Head-bowed, like a willing ox I serve the children (横眉冷对千夫指，俯首甘为巴子牛)” vividly portrays his personality.","chunks":["Born in 1881 in Shaoxing, Zhejiang Province, Lu Xun, also known as Zhou Shuren, was a great thinker and a revolutionary figure in literature. He was one of the founders of modern Chinese literature, using literature and art as weapons to transform the national spirit and character. His line, “Fierce-browed, I coolly defy a thousand pointing fingers; Head-bowed, like a willing ox I serve the children (横眉冷对千夫指，俯首甘为巴子牛)” vividly portrays his personality."]},{"id":"u2_s2_p2","buf":["Adopting the concise and flexible style of foreign fiction, Lu Xun broke away from the restrictive forms of traditional Chinese fiction and created a new form of modern Chinese fiction. He wrote stories, poems, essays, literary criticism, and literary history."],"text":"Adopting the concise and flexible style of foreign fiction, Lu Xun broke away from the restrictive forms of traditional Chinese fiction and created a new form of modern Chinese fiction. He wrote stories, poems, essays, literary criticism, and literary history.","chunks":["Adopting the concise and flexible style of foreign fiction, Lu Xun broke away from the restrictive forms of traditional Chinese fiction and created a new form of modern Chinese fiction. He wrote stories, poems, essays, literary criticism, and literary history."]},{"id":"u2_s2_p3","buf":["His first story, A Madman’s Diary (《狂人日记》), was the first written in the modern form of Chinese literature, marking the beginning of modern Chinese fiction. His first collection of stories was Outcry (《呐喊》) which includes arguably his most celebrated story The True Story of Ah Q (《阿Q正传》), This story depicts a humble, aes Literature ignorant yet stubborn and defiant peasant. As an ordinary man, Ah Q experiences a series of humiliations (/4 4) with a complete lack of self-awareness and is ultimately executed during the chaos of the Revolution of 1911. Ah Q is considered the personification of the negative traits of the Chinese national character. The term Ah Q-ism (阿 Q 精神) was coined to describe the rationalisation of defeat as a “spiritual victory”. The writer hoped to awaken Chinese people’s awareness and desire for revolution."],"text":"His first story, A Madman’s Diary (《狂人日记》), was the first written in the modern form of Chinese literature, marking the beginning of modern Chinese fiction. His first collection of stories was Outcry (《呐喊》) which includes arguably his most celebrated story The True Story of Ah Q (《阿Q正传》), This story depicts a humble, aes Literature ignorant yet stubborn and defiant peasant. As an ordinary man, Ah Q experiences a series of humiliations (/4 4) with a complete lack of self-awareness and is ultimately executed during the chaos of the Revolution of 1911. Ah Q is considered the personification of the negative traits of the Chinese national character. The term Ah Q-ism (阿 Q 精神) was coined to describe the rationalisation of defeat as a “spiritual victory”. The writer hoped to awaken Chinese people’s awareness and desire for revolution.","chunks":["His first story, A Madman’s Diary (《狂人日记》), was the first written in the modern form of Chinese literature, marking the beginning of modern Chinese fiction. His first collection of stories was Outcry (《呐喊》) which includes arguably his most celebrated story The True Story of Ah Q (《阿Q正传》), This story depicts a humble, aes Literature ignorant yet stubborn and defiant peasant. As an ordinary man, Ah Q experiences a series of humiliations (/4 4) with a complete lack of self-awareness and is ultimately executed during the chaos of the Revolution of 1911. Ah Q is considered the personification of the negative traits of the Chinese national character. The term Ah Q-ism (阿 Q 精神) was coined to describe the rationalisation of defeat as a “spiritual victory”. The writer hoped to awaken Chinese people’s awareness and desire for revolution."]},{"id":"u2_s2_p4","buf":["Wandering (《彷徨》) was his second collection of stories. In his third collection Old Tales Retold (《故事新编》), he retold old Chinese stories from his own perspective. Another major work is Wild Grass (《野草》), a collection of prose poems. Lu Xun left behind a rich spiritual legacy for future generations. He authored three collections of short stories, seventeen collections of essays, one collection of prose poems, one collection of memoirs, as well as letters, diaries, and academic works. In addition, he translated thirty-three works, amounting to more than two and a half million words."],"text":"Wandering (《彷徨》) was his second collection of stories. In his third collection Old Tales Retold (《故事新编》), he retold old Chinese stories from his own perspective. Another major work is Wild Grass (《野草》), a collection of prose poems. Lu Xun left behind a rich spiritual legacy for future generations. He authored three collections of short stories, seventeen collections of essays, one collection of prose poems, one collection of memoirs, as well as letters, diaries, and academic works. In addition, he translated thirty-three works, amounting to more than two and a half million words.","chunks":["Wandering (《彷徨》) was his second collection of stories. In his third collection Old Tales Retold (《故事新编》), he retold old Chinese stories from his own perspective. Another major work is Wild Grass (《野草》), a collection of prose poems. Lu Xun left behind a rich spiritual legacy for future generations. He authored three collections of short stories, seventeen collections of essays, one collection of prose poems, one collection of memoirs, as well as letters, diaries, and academic works. In addition, he translated thirty-three works, amounting to more than two and a half million words."]}]},{"id":"u2_s3","title":"郭沫若（1892—1978）","paras":[{"id":"u2_s3_p1","buf":["Guo Moruo was born in Leshan County, Sichuan Province. He was a writer, poet, dramatist, historian, archaeologist, expert in palaeography (古文字学), and social activist."],"text":"Guo Moruo was born in Leshan County, Sichuan Province. He was a writer, poet, dramatist, historian, archaeologist, expert in palaeography (古文字学), and social activist.","chunks":["Guo Moruo was born in Leshan County, Sichuan Province. He was a writer, poet, dramatist, historian, archaeologist, expert in palaeography (古文字学), and social activist."]},{"id":"u2_s3_p2","buf":["The Goddess (《女神》), his first poetry anthology, unleashed (释放) his powerful imagination. In this collection, Guo transformed elements of the universe into poetic beings that served as animated objects of his emotional response. By expressing the desire to break the confines of feudalism, voicing strong demands for social reform, and exhibiting boundless enthusiasm for the pursuit of high ideals, The Goddess distinctly reflects the spirit of the May Fourth Movement."],"text":"The Goddess (《女神》), his first poetry anthology, unleashed (释放) his powerful imagination. In this collection, Guo transformed elements of the universe into poetic beings that served as animated objects of his emotional response. By expressing the desire to break the confines of feudalism, voicing strong demands for social reform, and exhibiting boundless enthusiasm for the pursuit of high ideals, The Goddess distinctly reflects the spirit of the May Fourth Movement.","chunks":["The Goddess (《女神》), his first poetry anthology, unleashed (释放) his powerful imagination. In this collection, Guo transformed elements of the universe into poetic beings that served as animated objects of his emotional response. By expressing the desire to break the confines of feudalism, voicing strong demands for social reform, and exhibiting boundless enthusiasm for the pursuit of high ideals, The Goddess distinctly reflects the spirit of the May Fourth Movement."]},{"id":"u2_s3_p3","buf":["His other representative works include Qu Yuan (《屈原》), Zhuo Wenjun (《卓文君》), and Wang Zhaojun (《王昭君》). His motivation for creating these historical plays was to make the past serve the present."],"text":"His other representative works include Qu Yuan (《屈原》), Zhuo Wenjun (《卓文君》), and Wang Zhaojun (《王昭君》). His motivation for creating these historical plays was to make the past serve the present.","chunks":["His other representative works include Qu Yuan (《屈原》), Zhuo Wenjun (《卓文君》), and Wang Zhaojun (《王昭君》). His motivation for creating these historical plays was to make the past serve the present."]}]},{"id":"u2_s4","title":"茅盾（1896—1981）","paras":[{"id":"u2_s4_p1","buf":["Mao Dun was a critic, novelist, and short-story writer noted for his commitment to realism. He captured the pulse of the times with a strong sense of responsibility, a sense of mission, and great political enthusiasm. Between 1920 and 1932, he was one of the chief editors of China’s foremost literary journal Fiction Monthly (《小说月报》). His acclaimed works include Midnight (《子夜》), Spring Silkworms (《春蚕》), and The Shop of the Lin Family (《林家铺子》)."],"text":"Mao Dun was a critic, novelist, and short-story writer noted for his commitment to realism. He captured the pulse of the times with a strong sense of responsibility, a sense of mission, and great political enthusiasm. Between 1920 and 1932, he was one of the chief editors of China’s foremost literary journal Fiction Monthly (《小说月报》). His acclaimed works include Midnight (《子夜》), Spring Silkworms (《春蚕》), and The Shop of the Lin Family (《林家铺子》).","chunks":["Mao Dun was a critic, novelist, and short-story writer noted for his commitment to realism. He captured the pulse of the times with a strong sense of responsibility, a sense of mission, and great political enthusiasm. Between 1920 and 1932, he was one of the chief editors of China’s foremost literary journal Fiction Monthly (《小说月报》). His acclaimed works include Midnight (《子夜》), Spring Silkworms (《春蚕》), and The Shop of the Lin Family (《林家铺子》)."]},{"id":"u2_s4_p2","buf":["Midnight is a product of Mao Dun’s profound understanding of life. It focuses on the characterisation of Wu Sunfu, whose image epitomises (是 …… 的缩影) the destiny of the Chinese national bourgeoisie (民族资产阶级)."],"text":"Midnight is a product of Mao Dun’s profound understanding of life. It focuses on the characterisation of Wu Sunfu, whose image epitomises (是 …… 的缩影) the destiny of the Chinese national bourgeoisie (民族资产阶级).","chunks":["Midnight is a product of Mao Dun’s profound understanding of life. It focuses on the characterisation of Wu Sunfu, whose image epitomises (是 …… 的缩影) the destiny of the Chinese national bourgeoisie (民族资产阶级)."]},{"id":"u2_s4_p3","buf":["Spring Silkworms tells the story of the everyday lives, aspirations, and struggles of an old peasant, Tongbao, and his family. Tongbao takes great care of his silkworms, but the armed conflicts caused by the Japanese invasion in Shanghai ruin his high hopes of selling the cocoons (蚕茧) at a good price."],"text":"Spring Silkworms tells the story of the everyday lives, aspirations, and struggles of an old peasant, Tongbao, and his family. Tongbao takes great care of his silkworms, but the armed conflicts caused by the Japanese invasion in Shanghai ruin his high hopes of selling the cocoons (蚕茧) at a good price.","chunks":["Spring Silkworms tells the story of the everyday lives, aspirations, and struggles of an old peasant, Tongbao, and his family. Tongbao takes great care of his silkworms, but the armed conflicts caused by the Japanese invasion in Shanghai ruin his high hopes of selling the cocoons (蚕茧) at a good price."]},{"id":"u2_s4_p4","buf":["The Shop of the Lin Family tells the story of the bankruptcy of the traditional commercial system as a result of foreign economic encroachment (蚕食)."],"text":"The Shop of the Lin Family tells the story of the bankruptcy of the traditional commercial system as a result of foreign economic encroachment (蚕食).","chunks":["The Shop of the Lin Family tells the story of the bankruptcy of the traditional commercial system as a result of foreign economic encroachment (蚕食)."]}]},{"id":"u2_s5","title":"巴金（1904—2005）","paras":[{"id":"u2_s5_p1","buf":["Ba Jin was born into an official’s family in Chengdu, Sichuan Province. His real name was Li Yaotang."],"text":"Ba Jin was born into an official’s family in Chengdu, Sichuan Province. His real name was Li Yaotang.","chunks":["Ba Jin was born into an official’s family in Chengdu, Sichuan Province. His real name was Li Yaotang."]},{"id":"u2_s5_p2","buf":["In 1927, he went to France, where he started his literary career and wrote his first novel, Destruction (《灭亡》), about a disillusioned young anarchist (无政府主义者). It is a tale of romance and revolution, reflecting Ba Jin’s sadness over China’s status in the world and his hope for revolution."],"text":"In 1927, he went to France, where he started his literary career and wrote his first novel, Destruction (《灭亡》), about a disillusioned young anarchist (无政府主义者). It is a tale of romance and revolution, reflecting Ba Jin’s sadness over China’s status in the world and his hope for revolution.","chunks":["In 1927, he went to France, where he started his literary career and wrote his first novel, Destruction (《灭亡》), about a disillusioned young anarchist (无政府主义者). It is a tale of romance and revolution, reflecting Ba Jin’s sadness over China’s status in the world and his hope for revolution."]},{"id":"u2_s5_p3","buf":["He returned to Shanghai in 1928 and became a full-time writer. His “Torrent Trilogy”(激流三部曲) established his reputation. The trilogy includes Family (《家》), Spring (《春》), and Autumn (《秋》). In these novels, he launched a fervent (强烈的) attack on the oppressive ethics of feudalism that had persisted in China for thousands of years."],"text":"He returned to Shanghai in 1928 and became a full-time writer. His “Torrent Trilogy”(激流三部曲) established his reputation. The trilogy includes Family (《家》), Spring (《春》), and Autumn (《秋》). In these novels, he launched a fervent (强烈的) attack on the oppressive ethics of feudalism that had persisted in China for thousands of years.","chunks":["He returned to Shanghai in 1928 and became a full-time writer. His “Torrent Trilogy”(激流三部曲) established his reputation. The trilogy includes Family (《家》), Spring (《春》), and Autumn (《秋》). In these novels, he launched a fervent (强烈的) attack on the oppressive ethics of feudalism that had persisted in China for thousands of years."]},{"id":"u2_s5_p4","buf":["He was elected head of the Chinese Writers’ Association in 1981, a post he held until he passed away on 17th October 2005, at the age of 101. He made outstanding contributions to modern Chinese literature."],"text":"He was elected head of the Chinese Writers’ Association in 1981, a post he held until he passed away on 17th October 2005, at the age of 101. He made outstanding contributions to modern Chinese literature.","chunks":["He was elected head of the Chinese Writers’ Association in 1981, a post he held until he passed away on 17th October 2005, at the age of 101. He made outstanding contributions to modern Chinese literature."]},{"id":"u2_s5_p5","buf":["There were also many other great writers and poets in the 20th century, including Lao She (1899—1966), known for works like Rickshaw Boy (《骆驼祥子》), Four Generations under One Roof(《四世同堂》), Teahouse (《茶馆》), and Dragon Beard Ditch (《龙须沟》); Cao Yu (1910—1996), a modern Chinese dramatist, known for Thunderstorm (《雷雨》) and Sunrise (《日出》); and Shen Congwen (1902—1988), with Border Town (《边城》) and Long River (《长河》) as his masterpieces."],"text":"There were also many other great writers and poets in the 20th century, including Lao She (1899—1966), known for works like Rickshaw Boy (《骆驼祥子》), Four Generations under One Roof(《四世同堂》), Teahouse (《茶馆》), and Dragon Beard Ditch (《龙须沟》); Cao Yu (1910—1996), a modern Chinese dramatist, known for Thunderstorm (《雷雨》) and Sunrise (《日出》); and Shen Congwen (1902—1988), with Border Town (《边城》) and Long River (《长河》) as his masterpieces.","chunks":["There were also many other great writers and poets in the 20th century, including Lao She (1899—1966), known for works like Rickshaw Boy (《骆驼祥子》), Four Generations under One Roof(《四世同堂》), Teahouse (《茶馆》), and Dragon Beard Ditch (《龙须沟》); Cao Yu (1910—1996), a modern Chinese dramatist, known for Thunderstorm (《雷雨》) and Sunrise (《日出》); and Shen Congwen (1902—1988), with Border Town (《边城》) and Long River (《长河》) as his masterpieces."]}]}]},{"id":"u3","title":"当代文学","sections":[{"id":"u3_s1","title":"当代文学","paras":[{"id":"u3_s1_p1","buf":["The starting point of contemporary Chinese literature was the First Congress of the All-China Literary and Art Workers, held in Beijing in July 1949, Contemporary Chinese literature, with the active efforts of writers and artists, and the active promotion of literary organisers and theoretical critics in different historical periods and different thematic fields, has created a large number of renowned works. These works are rich in themes and diverse in writing styles. They spread positive worldviews and values, while meeting the increasingly diverse aesthetic needs of the people."],"text":"The starting point of contemporary Chinese literature was the First Congress of the All-China Literary and Art Workers, held in Beijing in July 1949, Contemporary Chinese literature, with the active efforts of writers and artists, and the active promotion of literary organisers and theoretical critics in different historical periods and different thematic fields, has created a large number of renowned works. These works are rich in themes and diverse in writing styles. They spread positive worldviews and values, while meeting the increasingly diverse aesthetic needs of the people.","chunks":["The starting point of contemporary Chinese literature was the First Congress of the All-China Literary and Art Workers, held in Beijing in July 1949, Contemporary Chinese literature, with the active efforts of writers and artists, and the active promotion of literary organisers and theoretical critics in different historical periods and different thematic fields, has created a large number of renowned works. These works are rich in themes and diverse in writing styles. They spread positive worldviews and values, while meeting the increasingly diverse aesthetic needs of the people."]},{"id":"u3_s1_p2","buf":["Notable works from before the reform and opening up include Builders of a New Life by Liu Qing (柳青, 《创业史》), The Song of Youth by Yang Mo (杨沫 ,《青春之歌》), Tracks in the Snowy Forest by Qu Bo (曲波, 《林海雪原》), Keep the Red Flag Flying by Liang Bin (梁斌, 《红旗谱》), Red Sun by Wu Qiang (吴强 ,《红日》), and Red Crag by Luo Guangbin and Yang Yiyan (罗广斌、杨益言 ,《红岩》)."],"text":"Notable works from before the reform and opening up include Builders of a New Life by Liu Qing (柳青, 《创业史》), The Song of Youth by Yang Mo (杨沫 ,《青春之歌》), Tracks in the Snowy Forest by Qu Bo (曲波, 《林海雪原》), Keep the Red Flag Flying by Liang Bin (梁斌, 《红旗谱》), Red Sun by Wu Qiang (吴强 ,《红日》), and Red Crag by Luo Guangbin and Yang Yiyan (罗广斌、杨益言 ,《红岩》).","chunks":["Notable works from before the reform and opening up include Builders of a New Life by Liu Qing (柳青, 《创业史》), The Song of Youth by Yang Mo (杨沫 ,《青春之歌》), Tracks in the Snowy Forest by Qu Bo (曲波, 《林海雪原》), Keep the Red Flag Flying by Liang Bin (梁斌, 《红旗谱》), Red Sun by Wu Qiang (吴强 ,《红日》), and Red Crag by Luo Guangbin and Yang Yiyan (罗广斌、杨益言 ,《红岩》)."]},{"id":"u3_s1_p3","buf":["The Misty Poetry School (朦胧诗派) was born in the late 1970s and early 1980s. Representative poets include Bei Dao (北岛), Shu Ting (舒婷), Gu Cheng (顾城) Duoduo (多多), and Yang Lian (杨炼). They expressed themselves in deliberately obscure verses that reflected subjective realism."],"text":"The Misty Poetry School (朦胧诗派) was born in the late 1970s and early 1980s. Representative poets include Bei Dao (北岛), Shu Ting (舒婷), Gu Cheng (顾城) Duoduo (多多), and Yang Lian (杨炼). They expressed themselves in deliberately obscure verses that reflected subjective realism.","chunks":["The Misty Poetry School (朦胧诗派) was born in the late 1970s and early 1980s. Representative poets include Bei Dao (北岛), Shu Ting (舒婷), Gu Cheng (顾城) Duoduo (多多), and Yang Lian (杨炼). They expressed themselves in deliberately obscure verses that reflected subjective realism."]},{"id":"u3_s1_p4","buf":["A spirit of literary experimentation flourished in the second half of the 1980s. Wang Meng (王蒙), for example, was one of several writers who conducted explorations and experiments in the art of fiction. Another group of writers, part of the root-seeking literature movement, including Han Shaogong (韩少功) and Mo Yan (莫言), sought to reconnect literature and culture to Chinese traditions. Other writers, such as Yu Hua (余华) and Su Tong (苏童), experimented with a more avant-garde mode of writing, which was daring in form and language and displayed a complete loss of faith in ideals of any sort."],"text":"A spirit of literary experimentation flourished in the second half of the 1980s. Wang Meng (王蒙), for example, was one of several writers who conducted explorations and experiments in the art of fiction. Another group of writers, part of the root-seeking literature movement, including Han Shaogong (韩少功) and Mo Yan (莫言), sought to reconnect literature and culture to Chinese traditions. Other writers, such as Yu Hua (余华) and Su Tong (苏童), experimented with a more avant-garde mode of writing, which was daring in form and language and displayed a complete loss of faith in ideals of any sort.","chunks":["A spirit of literary experimentation flourished in the second half of the 1980s. Wang Meng (王蒙), for example, was one of several writers who conducted explorations and experiments in the art of fiction. Another group of writers, part of the root-seeking literature movement, including Han Shaogong (韩少功) and Mo Yan (莫言), sought to reconnect literature and culture to Chinese traditions. Other writers, such as Yu Hua (余华) and Su Tong (苏童), experimented with a more avant-garde mode of writing, which was daring in form and language and displayed a complete loss of faith in ideals of any sort."]},{"id":"u3_s1_p5","buf":["Wang Meng, born in Beijing in 1934, has published over 100 novels of varying lengths, three volumes of his autobiography, as well as essays, poems, reportage literature, biographies, literary criticism, and academic research. In 1956, Wang published The Young Newcomer in the Organisation Department (《组织部来了个年轻人》), which, along with Long Live Youth (《青春万岁》), became a classic."],"text":"Wang Meng, born in Beijing in 1934, has published over 100 novels of varying lengths, three volumes of his autobiography, as well as essays, poems, reportage literature, biographies, literary criticism, and academic research. In 1956, Wang published The Young Newcomer in the Organisation Department (《组织部来了个年轻人》), which, along with Long Live Youth (《青春万岁》), became a classic.","chunks":["Wang Meng, born in Beijing in 1934, has published over 100 novels of varying lengths, three volumes of his autobiography, as well as essays, poems, reportage literature, biographies, literary criticism, and academic research. In 1956, Wang published The Young Newcomer in the Organisation Department (《组织部来了个年轻人》), which, along with Long Live Youth (《青春万岁》), became a classic."]},{"id":"u3_s1_p6","buf":["Yu Hua, born in 1960 in Hangzhou, Zhejiang Province, practised dentistry for five years before turning to fiction writing in 1983."],"text":"Yu Hua, born in 1960 in Hangzhou, Zhejiang Province, practised dentistry for five years before turning to fiction writing in 1983.","chunks":["Yu Hua, born in 1960 in Hangzhou, Zhejiang Province, practised dentistry for five years before turning to fiction writing in 1983."]},{"id":"u3_s1_p7","buf":["To Live (《活着》) marks a significant shift in Yu Hua’s creative stance. The ordinary farmer Fugui, in the midst of the disasters of both the times and himself, experiences the successive deaths of his family members. The lonely old man resists the unknown hardships with his tenacious “living spirit”, demonstrating the resilience in enduring hardships and the tenderness in facing them. Chronicle of a Blood Merchant (《许三观卖血记》) further continues this style."],"text":"To Live (《活着》) marks a significant shift in Yu Hua’s creative stance. The ordinary farmer Fugui, in the midst of the disasters of both the times and himself, experiences the successive deaths of his family members. The lonely old man resists the unknown hardships with his tenacious “living spirit”, demonstrating the resilience in enduring hardships and the tenderness in facing them. Chronicle of a Blood Merchant (《许三观卖血记》) further continues this style.","chunks":["To Live (《活着》) marks a significant shift in Yu Hua’s creative stance. The ordinary farmer Fugui, in the midst of the disasters of both the times and himself, experiences the successive deaths of his family members. The lonely old man resists the unknown hardships with his tenacious “living spirit”, demonstrating the resilience in enduring hardships and the tenderness in facing them. Chronicle of a Blood Merchant (《许三观卖血记》) further continues this style."]},{"id":"u3_s1_p8","buf":["Su Tong, born in 1963 in Suzhou, began publishing novels in 1983 and became one of the most acclaimed novelists in China. Su’s works incorporate elements of avant-garde literature as well as resources from the genre of root-seeking literature. His notable works include Wives and Concubines (《妻妾成群》), Rice (《米》), My Life as Emperor (《我的帝王生涯》), and The Boat to Redemption (《河岸》). Wives and Concubines was adapted into the film Raise the Red Lantern (《大红灯笼高高挂》) by director Zhang Yimou."],"text":"Su Tong, born in 1963 in Suzhou, began publishing novels in 1983 and became one of the most acclaimed novelists in China. Su’s works incorporate elements of avant-garde literature as well as resources from the genre of root-seeking literature. His notable works include Wives and Concubines (《妻妾成群》), Rice (《米》), My Life as Emperor (《我的帝王生涯》), and The Boat to Redemption (《河岸》). Wives and Concubines was adapted into the film Raise the Red Lantern (《大红灯笼高高挂》) by director Zhang Yimou.","chunks":["Su Tong, born in 1963 in Suzhou, began publishing novels in 1983 and became one of the most acclaimed novelists in China. Su’s works incorporate elements of avant-garde literature as well as resources from the genre of root-seeking literature. His notable works include Wives and Concubines (《妻妾成群》), Rice (《米》), My Life as Emperor (《我的帝王生涯》), and The Boat to Redemption (《河岸》). Wives and Concubines was adapted into the film Raise the Red Lantern (《大红灯笼高高挂》) by director Zhang Yimou."]},{"id":"u3_s1_p9","buf":["At the turn of the 21st century, Chinese literature underwent great development. Chinese writers and literature continued to gain recognition in foreign circles. In 2012, Mo Yan became the first Chinese laureate of the Nobel Prize in Literature. Liu Cixin became the first Asian author to win the Hugo Award (雨果奖) for Best Novel for his book The Three-Body Problem (《三体》) in 2015. He led a renaissance in Chinese science fiction, exemplified by blockbuster adaptations of his works, such as The Wandering Earth (《流浪地球》) and Crazy Alien (《疯狂的外星人》)."],"text":"At the turn of the 21st century, Chinese literature underwent great development. Chinese writers and literature continued to gain recognition in foreign circles. In 2012, Mo Yan became the first Chinese laureate of the Nobel Prize in Literature. Liu Cixin became the first Asian author to win the Hugo Award (雨果奖) for Best Novel for his book The Three-Body Problem (《三体》) in 2015. He led a renaissance in Chinese science fiction, exemplified by blockbuster adaptations of his works, such as The Wandering Earth (《流浪地球》) and Crazy Alien (《疯狂的外星人》).","chunks":["At the turn of the 21st century, Chinese literature underwent great development. Chinese writers and literature continued to gain recognition in foreign circles. In 2012, Mo Yan became the first Chinese laureate of the Nobel Prize in Literature. Liu Cixin became the first Asian author to win the Hugo Award (雨果奖) for Best Novel for his book The Three-Body Problem (《三体》) in 2015. He led a renaissance in Chinese science fiction, exemplified by blockbuster adaptations of his works, such as The Wandering Earth (《流浪地球》) and Crazy Alien (《疯狂的外星人》)."]},{"id":"u3_s1_p10","buf":["Mo Yan (the pseudonym of Guan Moye), the first Chinese laureate of the Nobel Prize in Literature, was born in 1955 and grew up in Gaomi, Shandong Province. At the age of 12, he left school to work, first as a farmer, and later in a cotton factory. In 1976, he joined the People’s Liberation Army, where he began to study literature and write. In his writings, Mo Yan draws on his youthful experiences and the settings of his birthplace."],"text":"Mo Yan (the pseudonym of Guan Moye), the first Chinese laureate of the Nobel Prize in Literature, was born in 1955 and grew up in Gaomi, Shandong Province. At the age of 12, he left school to work, first as a farmer, and later in a cotton factory. In 1976, he joined the People’s Liberation Army, where he began to study literature and write. In his writings, Mo Yan draws on his youthful experiences and the settings of his birthplace.","chunks":["Mo Yan (the pseudonym of Guan Moye), the first Chinese laureate of the Nobel Prize in Literature, was born in 1955 and grew up in Gaomi, Shandong Province. At the age of 12, he left school to work, first as a farmer, and later in a cotton factory. In 1976, he joined the People’s Liberation Army, where he began to study literature and write. In his writings, Mo Yan draws on his youthful experiences and the settings of his birthplace."]},{"id":"u3_s1_p11","buf":["His notable works include Red Sorghum (《红高粱》) Big Breasts and Wide Hips (《丰乳肥臀》), Life and Death Are Wearing Me Out (《生死疲劳》), and Frog (《蛙》). Red Sorghum was successfully adapted into a film by director Zhang Yimou in 1987."],"text":"His notable works include Red Sorghum (《红高粱》) Big Breasts and Wide Hips (《丰乳肥臀》), Life and Death Are Wearing Me Out (《生死疲劳》), and Frog (《蛙》). Red Sorghum was successfully adapted into a film by director Zhang Yimou in 1987.","chunks":["His notable works include Red Sorghum (《红高粱》) Big Breasts and Wide Hips (《丰乳肥臀》), Life and Death Are Wearing Me Out (《生死疲劳》), and Frog (《蛙》). Red Sorghum was successfully adapted into a film by director Zhang Yimou in 1987."]},{"id":"u3_s1_p12","buf":["Through a mixture of fantasy and reality, as well as historical and social perspectives, Mo Yan creates a world reminiscent of the complexity found in the writings of William Faulkner and Gabriel Garcia Marquez. At the same time, he has found a departure point in traditional Chinese literature and oral tradition."],"text":"Through a mixture of fantasy and reality, as well as historical and social perspectives, Mo Yan creates a world reminiscent of the complexity found in the writings of William Faulkner and Gabriel Garcia Marquez. At the same time, he has found a departure point in traditional Chinese literature and oral tradition.","chunks":["Through a mixture of fantasy and reality, as well as historical and social perspectives, Mo Yan creates a world reminiscent of the complexity found in the writings of William Faulkner and Gabriel Garcia Marquez. At the same time, he has found a departure point in traditional Chinese literature and oral tradition."]}]}]}],"vocabA":[{"term":"The Spring and Autumn Annals","zh":"春秋","q":"• The Spring and Autumn Annals (《春秋》): It is the earliest chronicle (编年史) in China with concise and refined language.","tr":"• 《春秋》：它是中国最早的编年史，语言简练精当。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Dreaming of My Deceased Wife","zh":"江城子·乙卯正月二十日夜记梦","q":"Some of his most famous ci include “Memories of the Past at Red Cliff (to the tune of ‘Charm of a Maiden Singer’)” (《念奴娇·赤壁怀古》), “The Mid-Autumn Festival (to the tune of ‘Prelude to Water Melody )”(《水调歌头·明月几时有》), and “Dreaming of My Deceased Wife (to the tune of ‘A Riverside Town’)” (《江城子·乙卯正月二十日夜记梦》).","tr":"他最著名的词作包括《念奴娇·赤壁怀古》、《水调歌头·明月几时有》和《江城子·乙卯正月二十日夜记梦》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"The Peacock Flies Southeast","zh":"孔雀东南飞","q":"The most famous of these works is “The Peacock Flies Southeast” (《孔雀东南飞》), one of the longest ballads from the end of the Eastern Han Dynasty.","tr":"这些民歌语言自然，生动描绘了普通百姓（尤其是妇女）的生活。通过运用比喻和拟人，这些民歌中的故事栩栩如生。其中最著名的作品是《孔雀东南飞》，它是东汉末年最长的叙事诗之一，讲述了焦仲卿和刘兰芝这对年轻夫妇的悲剧故事，常被解读为对封建礼教的批判。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Song of Everlasting Regret","zh":"长恨歌","q":"His most popular poems are the long narrative poems “Song of Everlasting Regret” (《长恨歌》) and “Song of a Pipa Player” (《琵琶行》).","tr":"他最受欢迎的诗作是长篇叙事诗《长恨歌》和《琵琶行》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Fu of Eulogy on Qu Yuan","zh":"吊屈原赋","q":"Jia’s representative works are “Fu of Eulogy on Qu Yuan”(《吊屈原赋》) and “Funiao Fu” (《鵩鸟赋》).","tr":"贾谊的代表作有《吊屈原赋》和《鵩鸟赋》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Hard Is the Road to Shu","zh":"蜀道难","q":"Some of Li Bai’s most famous poems include “Quiet Night Thoughts” (《静夜思》), “Hard Is the Road to Shu” (《蜀道难》), and “A Visit to Tianmu Mountain ina Dream” (《梦游天姥吟留别》).","tr":"李白最著名的诗作包括《静夜思》、《蜀道难》和《梦游天姥吟留别》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"The Old Charcoal Seller","zh":"卖炭翁","q":"For example, “The Old Charcoal Seller” (《卖炭翁》) is a lament against official oppression.","tr":"例如，《卖炭翁》是对官吏压迫的哀叹。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"The Mid-Autumn Festival","zh":"水调歌头·明月几时有","q":"Some of his most famous ci include “Memories of the Past at Red Cliff (to the tune of ‘Charm of a Maiden Singer’)” (《念奴娇·赤壁怀古》), “The Mid-Autumn Festival (to the tune of ‘Prelude to Water Melody )”(《水调歌头·明月几时有》), and “Dreaming of My Deceased Wife (to the tune of ‘A Riverside Town’)” (《江城子·乙卯正月二十日夜记梦》).","tr":"他最著名的词作包括《念奴娇·赤壁怀古》、《水调歌头·明月几时有》和《江城子·乙卯正月二十日夜记梦》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"A Dream of Red Mansions","zh":"红楼梦","q":"The Ming and Qing dynasties saw the publication of the Four Great Classical Novels: Romance of the Three Kingdoms (《三国演义》), Water Margin (《水浒传》), Journey to the West (《西游记》), and A Dream of Red Mansions (《红楼梦》).","tr":"明清时期出版了中国四大古典名著：《三国演义》、《水浒传》、《西游记》和《红楼梦》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"The Injustice to Dou E","zh":"窦娥冤","q":"Guan Hanging, the most highly-reputed dramatist of the period, wrote the masterpiece tragedy “The Injustice to Dou E” (《窦娥冤》).","tr":"关汉卿是这一时期声誉最高的剧作家，创作了名作悲剧《窦娥冤》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Song of a Pipa Player","zh":"琵琶行","q":"His most popular poems are the long narrative poems “Song of Everlasting Regret” (《长恨歌》) and “Song of a Pipa Player” (《琵琶行》).","tr":"他最受欢迎的诗作是长篇叙事诗《长恨歌》和《琵琶行》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Peach Blossom Spring","zh":"桃花源记","q":"Tao’s masterpiece “Peach Blossom Spring”(《桃花源记》) is celebrated for its depiction of the secluded and ideal life people long for.","tr":"陶渊明的代表作《桃花源记》因描绘了人们所向往的幽静理想生活而备受赞誉。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Quiet Night Thoughts","zh":"静夜思","q":"Some of Li Bai’s most famous poems include “Quiet Night Thoughts” (《静夜思》), “Hard Is the Road to Shu” (《蜀道难》), and “A Visit to Tianmu Mountain ina Dream” (《梦游天姥吟留别》).","tr":"李白最著名的诗作包括《静夜思》、《蜀道难》和《梦游天姥吟留别》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Encountering Sorrow","zh":"离骚","q":"His immortal masterpieces include Encountering Sorrow (《离骚》), Questioning Heaven (《天问》), Nine Pieces (《九章》), and Nine Songs (《九歌》).","tr":"他不朽的代表作包括《离骚》、《天问》、《九章》和《九歌》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Journey to the West","zh":"西游记","q":"The Ming and Qing dynasties saw the publication of the Four Great Classical Novels: Romance of the Three Kingdoms (《三国演义》), Water Margin (《水浒传》), Journey to the West (《西游记》), and A Dream of Red Mansions (《红楼梦》).","tr":"明清时期出版了中国四大古典名著：《三国演义》、《水浒传》、《西游记》和《红楼梦》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Questioning Heaven","zh":"天问","q":"His immortal masterpieces include Encountering Sorrow (《离骚》), Questioning Heaven (《天问》), Nine Pieces (《九章》), and Nine Songs (《九歌》).","tr":"他不朽的代表作包括《离骚》、《天问》、《九章》和《九歌》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Classic of History","zh":"尚书","q":"The formation of ancient Chinese prose is marked by Classic of History (《尚书》), a collection of documentary records of events in China’s ancient history.","tr":"中国古代散文的形成以《尚书》为标志，它是一部记录中国上古历史事件的文献汇编。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Water Dragon Chant","zh":"水龙吟·登建康赏心亭","q":"His representative works include “On the Riverside Tower at Jiankang (to the tune of “Water Dragon Chant’)” (《水龙吟·登建康赏心亭》) and “Thinking of Ancient Heroes in the Tower on the Northern Hill at Jingkou (to the tune of ‘Always Having Fun’)” (《永遇乐·京口北固亭怀古》).","tr":"他的代表作包括《水龙吟·登建康赏心亭》和《永遇乐·京口北固亭怀古》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"The Book of Songs","zh":"诗经","q":"Beginning with primitive mythology, Chinese classical literature developed through works such as The Book of Songs (《诗经》), Odes of Chu (《楚辞》), pre-Qin prose, fu (赋) and yuefu (乐府) poetry of the Han Dynasty, literary criticism in the Wei and Jin dynasties, Tang shi (poetry), Song ci (lyric), Yuan qu (元曲) or Yuan opera, and the novels of the Ming and Qing dynasties.","tr":"中国古典文学始于原始神话，经由《诗经》、《楚辞》、先秦散文、汉代的赋与乐府诗、魏晋时期的文学批评、唐诗、宋词、元曲以及明清小说等作品逐步发展。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Beautiful Lady Yu","zh":"虞美人","q":"The “Beautiful Lady Yu (《虞美人》)”, “The Moon on the West River (西江月)”“Butterflies in Love with Flowers (《蝶恋花》) and “Immortals at the Magpie Bridge (《鹊桥仙》)” are all notable examples of cipai.","tr":"《虞美人》、《西江月》、《蝶恋花》和《鹊桥仙》都是词牌的著名例子。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Slow, Slow Song","zh":"声声慢","q":"Her famous ci “Slow, Slow Song” (《声声慢》) conveys her intense grief and loneliness during a time of personal and national turmoil.","tr":"她的名作《声声慢》传达了她身处个人与国家动荡时期强烈的悲恸与孤独。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Autumn Thoughts","zh":"天净沙·秋思","q":"Ma Zhiyuan’s “Autumn Thoughts (to the tune of ‘Heavenly Pure Sand’)” (《天净沙·秋思》), a xiaoling, enjoys great popularity.","tr":"散曲与诗的其他形式一样，描写景物、情感或事件，可供歌唱；但它不包含说白或供表演者使用的舞台提示。散曲的形式包括小令（短小的曲调）、散套（同一宫调的若干支散曲联成的组曲）和带过曲（由两三个曲调组成的小型套曲）。马致远的《天净沙·秋思》是一支小令，广受欢迎。马致远这支28字的作品描绘了异乡的荒野，抒发了他的孤独与悲凉。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"On the Height","zh":"登高","q":"His poems, which span a wide range of styles, include masterpieces such as “On the Height” (《登高》) and “My Cottage Unroofed by Autumn Gales” (《茅屋为秋风所破歌》).","tr":"他的诗作风格多样，包括《登高》、《茅屋为秋风所破歌》等名篇。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"A Short Song","zh":"短歌行","q":"In “A Short Song” (《短歌行》), he directly references lines from The Book of Songs to express the urgent desire for talents and his ambition.","tr":"被称为“三曹”的群体包括曹操、曹丕和曹植；“建安七子”包括孔融、陈琳、王粲、徐干、阮瑀、应玚和刘桢。他们是杰出的文学家。他们的诗歌捕捉了时代精神，营造出英雄气概与悲凉感伤的氛围，并创造了一种后来被称为“建安风骨”的风格。曹操深受《诗经》影响。在《短歌行》中，他直接化用《诗经》的诗句，表达求贤若渴的急切心情和自己的抱负。《观沧海》是曹操的另一篇名作。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Water Margin","zh":"水浒传","q":"The Ming and Qing dynasties saw the publication of the Four Great Classical Novels: Romance of the Three Kingdoms (《三国演义》), Water Margin (《水浒传》), Journey to the West (《西游记》), and A Dream of Red Mansions (《红楼梦》).","tr":"明清时期出版了中国四大古典名著：《三国演义》、《水浒传》、《西游记》和《红楼梦》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"The Scholars","zh":"儒林外史","q":"The Scholars (《儒林外史》), authored by Wu Jingzi is alandmark colloquial and satirical novel in Chinese literature.","tr":"《儒林外史》由吴敬梓所著，是中国文学中一部里程碑式的白话讽刺小说。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Odes of Chu","zh":"楚辞","q":"Beginning with primitive mythology, Chinese classical literature developed through works such as The Book of Songs (《诗经》), Odes of Chu (《楚辞》), pre-Qin prose, fu (赋) and yuefu (乐府) poetry of the Han Dynasty, literary criticism in the Wei and Jin dynasties, Tang shi (poetry), Song ci (lyric), Yuan qu (元曲) or Yuan opera, and the novels of the Ming and Qing dynasties.","tr":"中国古典文学始于原始神话，经由《诗经》、《楚辞》、先秦散文、汉代的赋与乐府诗、魏晋时期的文学批评、唐诗、宋词、元曲以及明清小说等作品逐步发展。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Nine Pieces","zh":"九章","q":"His immortal masterpieces include Encountering Sorrow (《离骚》), Questioning Heaven (《天问》), Nine Pieces (《九章》), and Nine Songs (《九歌》).","tr":"他不朽的代表作包括《离骚》、《天问》、《九章》和《九歌》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Nine Songs","zh":"九歌","q":"His immortal masterpieces include Encountering Sorrow (《离骚》), Questioning Heaven (《天问》), Nine Pieces (《九章》), and Nine Songs (《九歌》).","tr":"他不朽的代表作包括《离骚》、《天问》、《九章》和《九歌》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Funiao Fu","zh":"鵩鸟赋","q":"Jia’s representative works are “Fu of Eulogy on Qu Yuan”(《吊屈原赋》) and “Funiao Fu” (《鵩鸟赋》).","tr":"贾谊的代表作有《吊屈原赋》和《鵩鸟赋》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"The Sea","zh":"观沧海","q":"“The Sea\"”(《观沧海》) is another well-known piece of Cao Cao.","tr":"被称为“三曹”的群体包括曹操、曹丕和曹植；“建安七子”包括孔融、陈琳、王粲、徐干、阮瑀、应玚和刘桢。他们是杰出的文学家。他们的诗歌捕捉了时代精神，营造出英雄气概与悲凉感伤的氛围，并创造了一种后来被称为“建安风骨”的风格。曹操深受《诗经》影响。在《短歌行》中，他直接化用《诗经》的诗句，表达求贤若渴的急切心情和自己的抱负。《观沧海》是曹操的另一篇名作。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Qu Yuan","zh":"屈原","q":"Liu Xiang (刘向) collected and compiled the works of the noted poet Qu Yuan and others into Odes of Chu, which was later annotated and expanded by Wang Yi (王逸).","tr":"刘向收集屈原等著名诗人的作品编成《楚辞》，后由王逸作注并增补。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Family","zh":"家","q":"The works of the former school are delicate and sentimental, often focusing on themes of family life and love, while those of the latter school are bold and expansive, often addressing major social issues like the fate of the nation.","tr":"前者的作品细腻多情，多聚焦于家庭生活与爱情题材；后者的作品则豪迈奔放，多涉及如国家命运等重大社会问题。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Spring","zh":"春","q":"The Book of Songs is the oldest existing collection of poems or songs, dating from the early Western Zhou Dynasty to the middle of the Spring and Autumn Period.","tr":"《诗经》是现存最古老的诗歌（或歌谣）总集，其作品年代上起西周初年，下至春秋中叶。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Autumn","zh":"秋","q":"The Book of Songs is the oldest existing collection of poems or songs, dating from the early Western Zhou Dynasty to the middle of the Spring and Autumn Period.","tr":"《诗经》是现存最古老的诗歌（或歌谣）总集，其作品年代上起西周初年，下至春秋中叶。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Thinking of Ancient Heroes in the Tower on the Northern Hill at Jingkou","zh":"永遇乐·京口北固亭怀古","q":"His representative works include “On the Riverside Tower at Jiankang (to the tune of “Water Dragon Chant’)” (《水龙吟·登建康赏心亭》) and “Thinking of Ancient Heroes in the Tower on the Northern Hill at Jingkou (to the tune of ‘Always Having Fun’)” (《永遇乐·京口北固亭怀古》).","tr":"他的代表作包括《水龙吟·登建康赏心亭》和《永遇乐·京口北固亭怀古》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"}],"vocabB":[{"term":"The Shop of the Lin Family","zh":"林家铺子","q":"His acclaimed works include Midnight (《子夜》), Spring Silkworms (《春蚕》), and The Shop of the Lin Family (《林家铺子》).","tr":"他的著名作品包括《子夜》、《春蚕》和《林家铺子》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Big Breasts and Wide Hips","zh":"丰乳肥臀","q":"His notable works include Red Sorghum (《红高粱》) Big Breasts and Wide Hips (《丰乳肥臀》), Life and Death Are Wearing Me Out (《生死疲劳》), and Frog (《蛙》).","tr":"他的著名作品包括《红高粱》、《丰乳肥臀》、《生死疲劳》和《蛙》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"The True Story of Ah Q","zh":"阿Q正传","q":"His first collection of stories was Outcry (《呐喊》) which includes arguably his most celebrated story The True Story of Ah Q (《阿Q正传》), This story depicts a humble, aes Literature ignorant yet stubborn and defiant peasant.","tr":"他的第一篇小说《狂人日记》是中国文学中第一篇以现代形式写成的作品，标志着中国现代小说的开端。他的第一部小说集是《呐喊》，其中收录了堪称他最著名的小说《阿Q正传》。这篇小说刻画了一个卑微、无知却固执倔强的农民。作为一个普通人，阿Q毫无自知之明地经历了一系列屈辱，最终在1911年辛亥革命的动荡中被处决。阿Q被认为是中国国民性中负面特征的化身。“阿Q精神”一词被造出来，用以形容将失败合理化为“精神胜利”的心态。作者希望以此唤醒中国人的觉醒和对革命的渴望。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"The Boat to Redemption","zh":"河岸","q":"His notable works include Wives and Concubines (《妻妾成群》), Rice (《米》), My Life as Emperor (《我的帝王生涯》), and The Boat to Redemption (《河岸》).","tr":"他的著名作品包括《妻妾成群》、《米》、《我的帝王生涯》和《河岸》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"The Three-Body Problem","zh":"三体","q":"Liu Cixin became the first Asian author to win the Hugo Award (雨果奖) for Best Novel for his book The Three-Body Problem (《三体》) in 2015.","tr":"2015年，刘慈欣凭借其作品《三体》成为首位获得雨果奖最佳长篇故事奖的亚洲作家。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Raise the Red Lantern","zh":"大红灯笼高高挂","q":"Wives and Concubines was adapted into the film Raise the Red Lantern (《大红灯笼高高挂》) by director Zhang Yimou.","tr":"《妻妾成群》被导演张艺谋改编成电影《大红灯笼高高挂》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Wives and Concubines","zh":"妻妾成群","q":"His notable works include Wives and Concubines (《妻妾成群》), Rice (《米》), My Life as Emperor (《我的帝王生涯》), and The Boat to Redemption (《河岸》).","tr":"他的著名作品包括《妻妾成群》、《米》、《我的帝王生涯》和《河岸》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"The Wandering Earth","zh":"流浪地球","q":"He led a renaissance in Chinese science fiction, exemplified by blockbuster adaptations of his works, such as The Wandering Earth (《流浪地球》) and Crazy Alien (《疯狂的外星人》).","tr":"他引领了中国科幻的复兴，其作品被改编为大热的影视作品（如《流浪地球》和《疯狂的外星人》），即为明证。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Dragon Beard Ditch","zh":"龙须沟","q":"There were also many other great writers and poets in the 20th century, including Lao She (1899—1966), known for works like Rickshaw Boy (《骆驼祥子》), Four Generations under One Roof(《四世同堂》), Teahouse (《茶馆》), and Dragon Beard Ditch (《龙须沟》); Cao Yu (1910—1996), a modern Chinese dramatist, known for Thunderstorm (《雷雨》) and Sunrise (《日出》); and Shen Congwen (1902—1988), with Border Town (《边城》) and Long River (《长河》) as his masterpieces.","tr":"20世纪还有许多其他伟大的作家和诗人，包括：老舍（1899—1966），以《骆驼祥子》、《四世同堂》、《茶馆》和《龙须沟》等作品著称；曹禺（1910—1996），中国现代剧作家，以《雷雨》和《日出》著称；沈从文（1902—1988），其代表作是《边城》和《长河》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"My Life as Emperor","zh":"我的帝王生涯","q":"His notable works include Wives and Concubines (《妻妾成群》), Rice (《米》), My Life as Emperor (《我的帝王生涯》), and The Boat to Redemption (《河岸》).","tr":"他的著名作品包括《妻妾成群》、《米》、《我的帝王生涯》和《河岸》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Old Tales Retold","zh":"故事新编","q":"In his third collection Old Tales Retold (《故事新编》), he retold old Chinese stories from his own perspective.","tr":"在他的第三部小说集《故事新编》中，他从自己的视角重新讲述了中国古代故事。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Spring Silkworms","zh":"春蚕","q":"His acclaimed works include Midnight (《子夜》), Spring Silkworms (《春蚕》), and The Shop of the Lin Family (《林家铺子》).","tr":"他的著名作品包括《子夜》、《春蚕》和《林家铺子》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Fiction Monthly","zh":"小说月报","q":"Between 1920 and 1932, he was one of the chief editors of China’s foremost literary journal Fiction Monthly (《小说月报》).","tr":"1920年至1932年间，他是中国最重要的文学期刊《小说月报》的主编之一。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Long Live Youth","zh":"青春万岁","q":"In 1956, Wang published The Young Newcomer in the Organisation Department (《组织部来了个年轻人》), which, along with Long Live Youth (《青春万岁》), became a classic.","tr":"1956年，王蒙发表了《组织部来了个年轻人》，该作与《青春万岁》一同成为经典。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Wang Zhaojun","zh":"王昭君","q":"His other representative works include Qu Yuan (《屈原》), Zhuo Wenjun (《卓文君》), and Wang Zhaojun (《王昭君》).","tr":"他的其他代表作包括《屈原》、《卓文君》和《王昭君》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Rickshaw Boy","zh":"骆驼祥子","q":"There were also many other great writers and poets in the 20th century, including Lao She (1899—1966), known for works like Rickshaw Boy (《骆驼祥子》), Four Generations under One Roof(《四世同堂》), Teahouse (《茶馆》), and Dragon Beard Ditch (《龙须沟》); Cao Yu (1910—1996), a modern Chinese dramatist, known for Thunderstorm (《雷雨》) and Sunrise (《日出》); and Shen Congwen (1902—1988), with Border Town (《边城》) and Long River (《长河》) as his masterpieces.","tr":"20世纪还有许多其他伟大的作家和诗人，包括：老舍（1899—1966），以《骆驼祥子》、《四世同堂》、《茶馆》和《龙须沟》等作品著称；曹禺（1910—1996），中国现代剧作家，以《雷雨》和《日出》著称；沈从文（1902—1988），其代表作是《边城》和《长河》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Thunderstorm","zh":"雷雨","q":"There were also many other great writers and poets in the 20th century, including Lao She (1899—1966), known for works like Rickshaw Boy (《骆驼祥子》), Four Generations under One Roof(《四世同堂》), Teahouse (《茶馆》), and Dragon Beard Ditch (《龙须沟》); Cao Yu (1910—1996), a modern Chinese dramatist, known for Thunderstorm (《雷雨》) and Sunrise (《日出》); and Shen Congwen (1902—1988), with Border Town (《边城》) and Long River (《长河》) as his masterpieces.","tr":"20世纪还有许多其他伟大的作家和诗人，包括：老舍（1899—1966），以《骆驼祥子》、《四世同堂》、《茶馆》和《龙须沟》等作品著称；曹禺（1910—1996），中国现代剧作家，以《雷雨》和《日出》著称；沈从文（1902—1988），其代表作是《边城》和《长河》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"The Goddess","zh":"女神","q":"The Goddess (《女神》), his first poetry anthology, unleashed (释放) his powerful imagination.","tr":"《女神》是他的第一部诗集，释放了他强大的想象力。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Zhuo Wenjun","zh":"卓文君","q":"His other representative works include Qu Yuan (《屈原》), Zhuo Wenjun (《卓文君》), and Wang Zhaojun (《王昭君》).","tr":"他的其他代表作包括《屈原》、《卓文君》和《王昭君》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Destruction","zh":"灭亡","q":"In 1927, he went to France, where he started his literary career and wrote his first novel, Destruction (《灭亡》), about a disillusioned young anarchist (无政府主义者).","tr":"1927年，他前往法国，在那里开始了文学生涯，并写下了他的第一部小说《灭亡》，讲述一个幻灭的年轻无政府主义者的故事。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"}],"vocabHF":[{"term":"Border Town","zh":"边城","q":"There were also many other great writers and poets in the 20th century, including Lao She (1899—1966), known for works like Rickshaw Boy (《骆驼祥子》), Four Generations under One Roof(《四世同堂》), Teahouse (《茶馆》), and Dragon Beard Ditch (《龙须沟》); Cao Yu (1910—1996), a modern Chinese dramatist, known for Thunderstorm (《雷雨》) and Sunrise (《日出》); and Shen Congwen (1902—1988), with Border Town (《边城》) and Long River (《长河》) as his masterpieces.","tr":"20世纪还有许多其他伟大的作家和诗人，包括：老舍（1899—1966），以《骆驼祥子》、《四世同堂》、《茶馆》和《龙须沟》等作品著称；曹禺（1910—1996），中国现代剧作家，以《雷雨》和《日出》著称；沈从文（1902—1988），其代表作是《边城》和《长河》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Crazy Alien","zh":"疯狂的外星人","q":"He led a renaissance in Chinese science fiction, exemplified by blockbuster adaptations of his works, such as The Wandering Earth (《流浪地球》) and Crazy Alien (《疯狂的外星人》).","tr":"他引领了中国科幻的复兴，其作品被改编为大热的影视作品（如《流浪地球》和《疯狂的外星人》），即为明证。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Red Sorghum","zh":"红高粱","q":"His notable works include Red Sorghum (《红高粱》) Big Breasts and Wide Hips (《丰乳肥臀》), Life and Death Are Wearing Me Out (《生死疲劳》), and Frog (《蛙》).","tr":"他的著名作品包括《红高粱》、《丰乳肥臀》、《生死疲劳》和《蛙》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Wild Grass","zh":"野草","q":"Another major work is Wild Grass (《野草》), a collection of prose poems.","tr":"另一部重要作品是散文诗集《野草》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Long River","zh":"长河","q":"There were also many other great writers and poets in the 20th century, including Lao She (1899—1966), known for works like Rickshaw Boy (《骆驼祥子》), Four Generations under One Roof(《四世同堂》), Teahouse (《茶馆》), and Dragon Beard Ditch (《龙须沟》); Cao Yu (1910—1996), a modern Chinese dramatist, known for Thunderstorm (《雷雨》) and Sunrise (《日出》); and Shen Congwen (1902—1988), with Border Town (《边城》) and Long River (《长河》) as his masterpieces.","tr":"20世纪还有许多其他伟大的作家和诗人，包括：老舍（1899—1966），以《骆驼祥子》、《四世同堂》、《茶馆》和《龙须沟》等作品著称；曹禺（1910—1996），中国现代剧作家，以《雷雨》和《日出》著称；沈从文（1902—1988），其代表作是《边城》和《长河》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Wandering","zh":"彷徨","q":"Wandering (《彷徨》) was his second collection of stories.","tr":"《彷徨》是他的第二部小说集。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Midnight","zh":"子夜","q":"His acclaimed works include Midnight (《子夜》), Spring Silkworms (《春蚕》), and The Shop of the Lin Family (《林家铺子》).","tr":"他的著名作品包括《子夜》、《春蚕》和《林家铺子》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Teahouse","zh":"茶馆","q":"There were also many other great writers and poets in the 20th century, including Lao She (1899—1966), known for works like Rickshaw Boy (《骆驼祥子》), Four Generations under One Roof(《四世同堂》), Teahouse (《茶馆》), and Dragon Beard Ditch (《龙须沟》); Cao Yu (1910—1996), a modern Chinese dramatist, known for Thunderstorm (《雷雨》) and Sunrise (《日出》); and Shen Congwen (1902—1988), with Border Town (《边城》) and Long River (《长河》) as his masterpieces.","tr":"20世纪还有许多其他伟大的作家和诗人，包括：老舍（1899—1966），以《骆驼祥子》、《四世同堂》、《茶馆》和《龙须沟》等作品著称；曹禺（1910—1996），中国现代剧作家，以《雷雨》和《日出》著称；沈从文（1902—1988），其代表作是《边城》和《长河》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Sunrise","zh":"日出","q":"There were also many other great writers and poets in the 20th century, including Lao She (1899—1966), known for works like Rickshaw Boy (《骆驼祥子》), Four Generations under One Roof(《四世同堂》), Teahouse (《茶馆》), and Dragon Beard Ditch (《龙须沟》); Cao Yu (1910—1996), a modern Chinese dramatist, known for Thunderstorm (《雷雨》) and Sunrise (《日出》); and Shen Congwen (1902—1988), with Border Town (《边城》) and Long River (《长河》) as his masterpieces.","tr":"20世纪还有许多其他伟大的作家和诗人，包括：老舍（1899—1966），以《骆驼祥子》、《四世同堂》、《茶馆》和《龙须沟》等作品著称；曹禺（1910—1996），中国现代剧作家，以《雷雨》和《日出》著称；沈从文（1902—1988），其代表作是《边城》和《长河》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"To Live","zh":"活着","q":"To Live (《活着》) marks a significant shift in Yu Hua’s creative stance.","tr":"《活着》标志着余华创作立场的重大转变。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Outcry","zh":"呐喊","q":"His first collection of stories was Outcry (《呐喊》) which includes arguably his most celebrated story The True Story of Ah Q (《阿Q正传》), This story depicts a humble, aes Literature ignorant yet stubborn and defiant peasant.","tr":"他的第一篇小说《狂人日记》是中国文学中第一篇以现代形式写成的作品，标志着中国现代小说的开端。他的第一部小说集是《呐喊》，其中收录了堪称他最著名的小说《阿Q正传》。这篇小说刻画了一个卑微、无知却固执倔强的农民。作为一个普通人，阿Q毫无自知之明地经历了一系列屈辱，最终在1911年辛亥革命的动荡中被处决。阿Q被认为是中国国民性中负面特征的化身。“阿Q精神”一词被造出来，用以形容将失败合理化为“精神胜利”的心态。作者希望以此唤醒中国人的觉醒和对革命的渴望。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Rice","zh":"米","q":"His notable works include Wives and Concubines (《妻妾成群》), Rice (《米》), My Life as Emperor (《我的帝王生涯》), and The Boat to Redemption (《河岸》).","tr":"他的著名作品包括《妻妾成群》、《米》、《我的帝王生涯》和《河岸》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"Frog","zh":"蛙","q":"His notable works include Red Sorghum (《红高粱》) Big Breasts and Wide Hips (《丰乳肥臀》), Life and Death Are Wearing Me Out (《生死疲劳》), and Frog (《蛙》).","tr":"他的著名作品包括《红高粱》、《丰乳肥臀》、《生死疲劳》和《蛙》。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"},{"term":"The Young Newcomer in the Organisation Department","zh":"组织部来了个年轻人","q":"In 1956, Wang published The Young Newcomer in the Organisation Department (《组织部来了个年轻人》), which, along with Long Live Youth (《青春万岁》), became a classic.","tr":"1956年，王蒙发表了《组织部来了个年轻人》，该作与《青春万岁》一同成为经典。","ptype":"专有名词·篇名"}],"examsA":[{"term":"the Han Dynasty","zh":"汉朝","cn":"在汉朝时期，中国的版图大为扩展，经济繁荣，文化昌盛。（C6 2015·06·1）","en":"During the Han Dynasty, China's territory expanded greatly, and its economy prospered and its culture flourished.","ptype":"专有名词·译名"},{"term":"territory","zh":"版图","cn":"在汉朝时期，中国的版图大为扩展，经济繁荣，文化昌盛。（C6 2015·06·1）","en":"During the Han Dynasty, China's territory expanded greatly, and its economy prospered and its culture flourished.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"the Silk Road","zh":"丝绸之路","cn":"汉朝开辟了丝绸之路，促进了东西方的经济和文化交流。（C6 2015·06·1）","en":"The Han Dynasty opened the Silk Road, promoting economic and cultural exchanges between the East and the West.","ptype":"专有名词·译名"},{"term":"the East and the West","zh":"东西方","cn":"汉朝开辟了丝绸之路，促进了东西方的经济和文化交流。（C6 2015·06·1）","en":"The Han Dynasty opened the Silk Road, promoting economic and cultural exchanges between the East and the West.","ptype":"专有名词·译名"},{"term":"economic and cultural exchanges","zh":"经济和文化交流","cn":"汉朝开辟了丝绸之路，促进了东西方的经济和文化交流。（C6 2015·06·1）","en":"The Han Dynasty opened the Silk Road, promoting economic and cultural exchanges between the East and the West.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"institutions","zh":"制度","cn":"汉朝的许多制度和传统对后世产生了深远影响。（C6 2015·06·1）","en":"Many institutions and traditions of the Han Dynasty exerted a profound influence on later generations.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"later generations","zh":"后世","cn":"汉朝的许多制度和传统对后世产生了深远影响。（C6 2015·06·1）","en":"Many institutions and traditions of the Han Dynasty exerted a profound influence on later generations.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"urbanization","zh":"城市化","cn":"随着城市化和财富的积累，艺术和文学也蓬勃发展。（C6 2017·06·1）","en":"As urbanization advanced and wealth accumulated, art and literature also flourished.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"the accumulation of wealth","zh":"财富的积累","cn":"随着城市化和财富的积累，艺术和文学也蓬勃发展。（C6 2017·06·1）","en":"As urbanization advanced and wealth accumulated, art and literature also flourished.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"art and literature","zh":"艺术和文学","cn":"随着城市化和财富的积累，艺术和文学也蓬勃发展。（C6 2017·06·1）","en":"As urbanization advanced and wealth accumulated, art and literature also flourished.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"Li Bai","zh":"李白","cn":"李白、杜甫等著名诗人以简洁精炼的语言创作诗歌。（C6 2017·06·1）","en":"Famous poets such as Li Bai and Du Fu wrote poems in a simple and refined language.","ptype":"专有名词·译名"},{"term":"Du Fu","zh":"杜甫","cn":"李白、杜甫等著名诗人以简洁精炼的语言创作诗歌。（C6 2017·06·1）","en":"Famous poets such as Li Bai and Du Fu wrote poems in a simple and refined language.","ptype":"专有名词·译名"},{"term":"poetry","zh":"诗歌","cn":"李白、杜甫等著名诗人以简洁精炼的语言创作诗歌。（C6 2017·06·1）","en":"Famous poets such as Li Bai and Du Fu wrote poems in a simple and refined language.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"simple and refined","zh":"简洁精炼","cn":"李白、杜甫等著名诗人以简洁精炼的语言创作诗歌。（C6 2017·06·1）","en":"Famous poets such as Li Bai and Du Fu wrote poems in a simple and refined language.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"scholars","zh":"学者","cn":"他们的诗歌打动了从学者到普通大众的每一个人。（C6 2017·06·1）","en":"Their poems moved everyone, from scholars to the general public.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"the general public","zh":"普通大众","cn":"他们的诗歌打动了从学者到普通大众的每一个人。（C6 2017·06·1）","en":"Their poems moved everyone, from scholars to the general public.","ptype":"名词·搭配"}],"examsB":[{"term":"an inseparable part of","zh":"不可分割的一部分","cn":"方言被认为是当地文化不可分割的一部分，但近年来会说方言的人数一直在减少。（C6 2019·06·2）","en":"Dialects are considered an inseparable part of local culture, but in recent years the number of people who speak dialects has been declining.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"in recent years","zh":"|近年来","cn":"方言被认为是当地文化不可分割的一部分，但近年来会说方言的人数一直在减少。（C6 2019·06·2）","en":"Dialects are considered an inseparable part of local culture, but in recent years the number of people who speak dialects has been declining.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"those cultures","zh":"|当地文化","cn":"方言被认为是当地文化不可分割的一部分，但近年来会说方言的人数一直在减少。（C6 2019·06·2）","en":"Dialects are considered an inseparable part of local culture, but in recent years the number of people who speak dialects has been declining.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"cultural products","zh":"文化产品","cn":"越来越多的中国文化产品出现在全球市场，并逐渐受到海外消费者的青睐。（C6 2023·06·2）","en":"A growing number of Chinese cultural products are appearing on the global market and are favored by overseas consumers.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"overseas consumers","zh":"|海外消费者","cn":"越来越多的中国文化产品出现在全球市场，并逐渐受到海外消费者的青睐。（C6 2023·06·2）","en":"A growing number of Chinese cultural products are appearing on the global market and are favored by overseas consumers.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"global market","zh":"|全球市场","cn":"越来越多的中国文化产品出现在全球市场，并逐渐受到海外消费者的青睐。（C6 2023·06·2）","en":"A growing number of Chinese cultural products are appearing on the global market and are favored by overseas consumers.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"be favored by","zh":"|青睐","cn":"越来越多的中国文化产品出现在全球市场，并逐渐受到海外消费者的青睐。（C6 2023·06·2）","en":"A growing number of Chinese cultural products are appearing on the global market and are favored by overseas consumers.","ptype":"名词·搭配"},{"term":"a growing number of","zh":"|越来越多","cn":"越来越多的中国文化产品出现在全球市场，并逐渐受到海外消费者的青睐。（C6 2023·06·2）","en":"A growing number of Chinese cultural products are appearing on the global market and are favored by overseas consumers.","ptype":"名词·搭配"}],"exercises":[{"sec":"Comprehension 理解 · Matching 作品配对","kind":"fill","intro":"Match the following works with their writers and the periods in which they were written or published.","qs":[{"no":1,"q":"Fu of Eulogy on Qu Yuan (《吊屈原赋》) — writer: ______ · period: ______","ans":"Jia Yi；Han Dynasty","src":"教材 Exercises 1"},{"no":2,"q":"Song of Everlasting Regret (《长恨歌》) — writer: ______ · period: ______","ans":"Bai Juyi；Tang Dynasty","src":"教材 Exercises 1"},{"no":3,"q":"The Scholars (《儒林外史》) — writer: ______ · period: ______","ans":"Wu Jingzi；Qing Dynasty","src":"教材 Exercises 1"},{"no":4,"q":"The Goddess (《女神》) — writer: ______ · period: ______","ans":"Guo Moruo；modern period","src":"教材 Exercises 1"},{"no":5,"q":"Wives and Concubines (《妻妾成群》) — writer: ______ · period: ______","ans":"Su Tong；contemporary period","src":"教材 Exercises 1"}]},{"sec":"Comprehension 理解 · True or False 判断正误","kind":"fill","intro":"Decide whether the following statements are true (T) or false (F). 依据课文判断正误，填入 T 或 F。","qs":[{"no":1,"q":"______ Chinese classical literature refers to literary works from the days before the Qin Dynasty to the late Qing Dynasty.","ans":"F","src":"课文 u1_s2_p1：from the period before the Qin Dynasty up to 1919"},{"no":2,"q":"______ The Book of Songs is a collection of China's 305 oldest poems written in the Spring and Autumn period.","ans":"F","src":"课文 u1_s3_p2：dating from the early Western Zhou Dynasty to the middle of the Spring and Autumn Period"},{"no":3,"q":"______ All of yuefu poems are written in five-character lines.","ans":"F","src":"课文 u1_s7_p10：mostly … although some use seven-character or multi-character lines"},{"no":4,"q":"______ The Literary Mind and the Carving of Dragons by Liu Xie was intended to advocate the presence of social and political content in literature while criticising the efforts to seek the beauty of form at the cost of meaning.","ans":"T","src":"课文 u1_s8_p4"},{"no":5,"q":"______ Li Bai has long been considered the greatest poet of realism in Chinese literature.","ans":"F","src":"课文：李白为浪漫主义（Poet Immortal），现实主义为《诗经》/杜甫"},{"no":6,"q":"______ In his novels, Ba Jin aimed at attacking the oppressive ethics of feudalism.","ans":"T","src":"课文 u2_s5_p3"}]},{"sec":"Translation 术语翻译","kind":"table","intro":"Translate the following terms into English according to the reading text.","table":{"head":["Term","Translation"],"rows":[["四大名著","the Four Great Classical Novels"],["寻根文学","root-seeking literature"]]},"src":"教材 Exercises · Term Translation（英译沿用课文用词）"},{"sec":"Passage Translation 段落翻译 ①《诗经》","kind":"passage","intro":"Translate the following passage into English.","cn":"《诗经》是中国第一部诗歌总集，共入编了从西周初年至春秋中叶的305篇诗歌。这些诗歌可以划分为三类：风、雅、颂。《诗经》中的诗歌多以四言为主，表现手法主要是赋、比、兴。作为中国文学的起点，《诗经》对后代文学的影响非常深远。","ans":"The Book of Songs is the first collection of poetry in China, comprising 305 poems dating from the early Western Zhou Dynasty to the middle of the Spring and Autumn Period. These poems can be divided into three categories: feng (ballads), ya (court hymns) and song (eulogies). Most of the poems in The Book of Songs are written in four-character lines, and the main techniques of expression are fu (narrative), bi (analogy) and xing (association). As the starting point of Chinese literature, The Book of Songs has exerted a profound influence on later literature.","src":"教材 Exercises · Passage Translation 1"},{"sec":"Passage Translation 段落翻译 ②《论语》","kind":"passage","intro":"Translate the following passage into English.","cn":"《论语》是儒家经典之一，主要以语录和对话文体的形式记录了孔子及其弟子的言行，集中体现了孔子及儒家学派的政治主张、伦理思想、道德观念和教育原则等。《论语》成书于战国前期。现存《论语》共20篇，492章。","ans":"The Analects is one of the Confucian classics. Mainly in the form of quotations and dialogues, it records the sayings and deeds of Confucius and his disciples, and gives concentrated expression to the political views, ethical thought, moral concepts and educational principles of Confucius and the Confucian school. The Analects was completed in the early Warring States Period. The extant Analects consists of 20 books and 492 chapters.","src":"教材 Exercises · Passage Translation 2"},{"sec":"Critical Thinking and Discussion 思辨与讨论","kind":"open","intro":"Discuss and answer in English.","qs":[{"no":1,"q":"In 2015, The Three-Body Problem became the first Asian novel to win the prestigious Hugo Award. From a literary perspective, what are the main characteristics of the novel?","ans":"（开放题）示例：以宏大宇宙尺度与严密科学推演为骨架，把物理学设定与人类社会伦理并置；用中国历史经验（如“文革”背景）追问文明存续的代价；在科幻类型中融入寓言性叙事，提出“黑暗森林”式的宇宙社会学假设。","src":"教材 Exercises · Critical Thinking and Discussion"}]},{"sec":"Communication 口语任务","kind":"open","intro":"Work in groups and present your introduction.","qs":[{"no":1,"q":"You are a member of a reading club that includes both Chinese and international students. It is your turn to introduce one of your favourite Chinese writers to the group. Your introduction should cover: 1) the time in which they lived, 2) their life experiences, 3) their writing style / influences, 4) their representative works.","ans":"（开放题）示例：介绍苏轼——北宋人，一生宦海浮沉、三度贬谪；诗、词、文、书、画皆精，开豪放词风，以旷达化解逆境；代表作《念奴娇·赤壁怀古》《水调歌头·明月几时有》。","src":"教材 Exercises · Communication"}]}]};